It's an open shrimp toast,
like an open sandwich.

717
00:28:51,229 --> 00:28:54,566
I thought you had to fry that
in low heat for a long time.

718
00:28:54,632 --> 00:28:56,201
The temperature is fine.

719
00:28:56,267 --> 00:28:58,336
-Brioche will burn easily.
-Really?

720
00:28:58,403 --> 00:29:01,139
It's made with lots of butter,
so it'll burn easily.

721
00:29:01,206 --> 00:29:03,007
-It's okay.
-The oil seems hot.

722
00:29:03,074 --> 00:29:04,008
The temperature is fine.

723
00:29:04,075 --> 00:29:05,577
Chinese-style brunch.

724
00:29:06,578 --> 00:29:07,912
CHECKING ON THE STEAK

725
00:29:07,979 --> 00:29:09,714
The meat.

726
00:29:09,781 --> 00:29:11,349
PULL IT OUT AND LET IT REST!

727
00:29:11,416 --> 00:29:13,518
I guess he's using steak for the parfait.

728
00:29:13,585 --> 00:29:14,519
How's she going to eat it?

729
00:29:14,586 --> 00:29:16,721
Eleven layers? How's that possible?

730
00:29:17,188 --> 00:29:20,125
GARLIC & ONION

731
00:29:20,191 --> 00:29:23,862
What's he doing with butter, garlic,
and white wine? Is it the sauce?

732
00:29:24,496 --> 00:29:26,498
-Tomato sauce.
-She had bought it to cook.

733
00:29:26,564 --> 00:29:28,833
-You got that to cook for yourself.
-My tomato sauce.

734
00:29:28,900 --> 00:29:30,235
-That sauce.
-It's finally getting used.

735
00:29:30,535 --> 00:29:31,803
BEEP

736
00:29:31,870 --> 00:29:33,338
-What was that?
-Pasta.

737
00:29:33,404 --> 00:29:37,108
He gets the pasta out.
He used a timer for it.

738
00:29:37,175 --> 00:29:38,576
That'll go into the cup?

739
00:29:38,643 --> 00:29:40,879
-In the cup?
-It's the sucking dish.

740
00:29:40,945 --> 00:29:42,614
-That's The Gentleman Sucks.
-The Gentleman Sucks.

741
00:29:42,680 --> 00:29:44,582
-The Gentleman Sucks?
-The Gentleman Sucks?

742
00:29:44,949 --> 00:29:46,317
FOR THOSE WHO DON'T REMEMBER…

743
00:29:46,384 --> 00:29:48,887
I've made a noodle dish
that you can suck on.

744
00:29:48,953 --> 00:29:50,655
The Gentleman Sucks?

745
00:29:50,722 --> 00:29:52,724
It was The Gentleman Sucks.

746
00:29:52,791 --> 00:29:54,759
-Nine minutes.
-Nine minutes.

747
00:29:55,326 --> 00:29:56,961
I guess Chef Eun-yeong…

748
00:29:57,028 --> 00:29:59,464
-Octopus.
-…is going to stir-fry octopus.

749
00:29:59,531 --> 00:30:01,900
-Check on the meat.
-The meat?

750
00:30:01,966 --> 00:30:03,001
It might burn.

751
00:30:03,067 --> 00:30:04,469
QUICKLY FLIPS THEM

752
00:30:06,037 --> 00:30:08,406
Both dishes seem difficult to make.

753
00:30:08,473 --> 00:30:10,508
I'm running out of time.

754
00:30:10,809 --> 00:30:12,210
OCTOPUS GOES INTO THE PAN!

755
00:30:12,277 --> 00:30:13,711
What about the shrimp toast?

756
00:30:13,778 --> 00:30:15,280
-Check on the toast.
-The shrimp toast.

757
00:30:15,346 --> 00:30:16,181
Shrimp toast. One sec.

758
00:30:16,381 --> 00:30:18,249
SHE GRABBED A KNIFE
AND MUST CHOP THE ONION…

759
00:30:18,316 --> 00:30:20,218
-It's burnt?
-It already looks burnt.

760
00:30:20,285 --> 00:30:22,453
No, it's not burnt.

761
00:30:22,520 --> 00:30:23,588
-It's not?
-No.

762
00:30:23,655 --> 00:30:25,890
-Is it okay?
-It is. It's almost too good.

763
00:30:25,957 --> 00:30:29,627
-The chili paste her friend left behind.
-The chili paste.

764
00:30:29,694 --> 00:30:31,029
The old friend's chili paste.

765
00:30:31,095 --> 00:30:32,363
SPICY TOMATO SOUP

766
00:30:32,430 --> 00:30:34,799
The edges are slowly turning dark.

767
00:30:34,866 --> 00:30:37,936
I like <i>nurungji,</i>
but I try to stay away from burnt food.

768
00:30:38,002 --> 00:30:39,470
She doesn't like burnt food.

769
00:30:39,537 --> 00:30:42,040
-What now?
-She hates burnt food.

770
00:30:42,106 --> 00:30:44,676
-She didn't say she liked burnt food.
-It's not burnt.

771
00:30:44,742 --> 00:30:47,111
Pay close attention to the shrimp toast.

772
00:30:47,178 --> 00:30:49,247
It's a dish that she loves to eat.

773
00:30:49,314 --> 00:30:51,316
Is the meat okay?

774
00:30:51,382 --> 00:30:52,817
-Pork neck is rather fatty.
-It is.

775
00:30:52,884 --> 00:30:53,785
It burns easily.

776
00:30:53,852 --> 00:30:56,054
It's not easy to cook them just right.

777
00:30:56,120 --> 00:30:58,489
-It got burnt.
-The pork neck!

778
00:30:58,990 --> 00:31:00,024
EMERGENCY

779
00:31:00,091 --> 00:31:01,726
I told you that the meat was burning!

780
00:31:01,793 --> 00:31:02,927
It's burning her heart too.

781
00:31:02,994 --> 00:31:04,729
I'm not using this one.

782
00:31:04,796 --> 00:31:07,432
She threw it away.

783
00:31:07,498 --> 00:31:11,369
She's a tough girl
full of testosterone in her body.

784
00:31:11,436 --> 00:31:12,503
That's her.

785
00:31:12,570 --> 00:31:14,572
You have to keep flipping
when you broil meat.

786
00:31:14,639 --> 00:31:15,773
It looks so good.

787
00:31:16,174 --> 00:31:17,475
WHAT'S JONG-WON UP TO?

788
00:31:17,675 --> 00:31:19,310
CREAM CHEESE

789
00:31:20,445 --> 00:31:22,413
Can you whip this, Nam-no?

790
00:31:22,480 --> 00:31:23,715
What is he whipping?

791
00:31:23,781 --> 00:31:24,983
Cream cheese.

792
00:31:25,049 --> 00:31:28,019
-To make it soft.
-They'll clean out most of my fridge.

793
00:31:28,086 --> 00:31:28,920
That's nice.

794
00:31:28,987 --> 00:31:32,290
-Is it?
-I can now get a new batch of groceries.

795
00:31:32,357 --> 00:31:35,026
We're cleaning it out for you.

796
00:31:35,093 --> 00:31:37,028
-It looks soft.
-That's good.

797
00:31:37,695 --> 00:31:40,765
The yolks are almost red.

798
00:31:40,832 --> 00:31:42,567
You had a variety of salts,

799
00:31:42,634 --> 00:31:44,369
so I'll use truffle salt for the eggs.

800
00:31:44,435 --> 00:31:47,305
-Truffle salt.
-She has truffle salt?

801
00:31:47,372 --> 00:31:50,008
-Eggs go very well with truffle.
-Right.

802
00:31:50,575 --> 00:31:53,311
BACK TO HER GROWN WOMAN VIBE

803
00:31:54,245 --> 00:31:55,079
She had burrata.

804
00:31:55,146 --> 00:31:57,248
-She had burrata that huge?
-Burrata?

805
00:31:58,182 --> 00:31:59,350
Avocado.

806
00:31:59,417 --> 00:32:00,485
An avocado.

807
00:32:01,252 --> 00:32:03,221
It's not spoiled, right? I'm glad.

808
00:32:03,288 --> 00:32:05,690
-Does it look good?
-It's great.

809
00:32:05,757 --> 00:32:08,593
-They're both using avocados.
-Jong-won is using them too.

810
00:32:08,660 --> 00:32:10,194
They're all fine.

811
00:32:10,261 --> 00:32:13,064
I was worried because they were
already ripe when I got them.

812
00:32:13,131 --> 00:32:15,433
Avocado slices go on top of burrata.

813
00:32:15,500 --> 00:32:16,634
What's that?

814
00:32:16,701 --> 00:32:18,569
It's the appetizer.

815
00:32:18,636 --> 00:32:20,638
-It's probably the appetizer.
-The appetizer.

816
00:32:20,705 --> 00:32:22,440
-Yes.
-So before she starts her meal?

817
00:32:22,507 --> 00:32:23,541
Yes, before the meal.

818
00:32:23,608 --> 00:32:25,510
-That's a pre-meal dish?
-Yes.

819
00:32:25,576 --> 00:32:27,779
That's the appetizer.

820
00:32:27,845 --> 00:32:29,180
That looks delicious.

821
00:32:29,247 --> 00:32:32,350
Jong-won must have more work to do
because he has to make lots of things.

822
00:32:32,417 --> 00:32:35,086
-He has many fillings to make.
-He'll blend the avocados.

823
00:32:35,153 --> 00:32:36,955
He's moving very fast.

824
00:32:37,021 --> 00:32:37,956
WHIRRR

825
00:32:38,022 --> 00:32:40,091
It's not blending very well.

826
00:32:40,158 --> 00:32:41,592
Six minutes.

827
00:32:42,794 --> 00:32:43,828
FILLING 5: AVOCADO PURÉE

828
00:32:43,895 --> 00:32:44,729
Put this in here.

829
00:32:44,796 --> 00:32:47,398
-We should be seeing something.
-But there's nothing.

830
00:32:47,465 --> 00:32:48,466
THE TOOLMAN IS HARD AT WORK

831
00:32:48,967 --> 00:32:50,735
NEXT, MORE EGGS!

832
00:32:50,802 --> 00:32:52,870
Jong-won beats some eggs.

833
00:32:52,937 --> 00:32:54,238
3. MAKE CRÊPE BATTER

834
00:32:54,305 --> 00:32:57,575
He's doing something. He's making batter.

835
00:32:57,642 --> 00:33:00,078
I'm making crispy crêpe.

836
00:33:00,144 --> 00:33:02,714
He mixed egg yolks with something.

837
00:33:02,780 --> 00:33:04,983
They don't even have five minutes now.

838
00:33:05,049 --> 00:33:07,051
-Sung-ju, please report from the kitchen.
-Okay.

839
00:33:07,118 --> 00:33:09,187
I wouldn't have noticed
if it hadn't been for Sang-uk.

840
00:33:09,253 --> 00:33:11,255
You have less than five minutes.

841
00:33:11,322 --> 00:33:12,924
There's just not enough time.

842
00:33:12,991 --> 00:33:15,560
-That's crêpe?
-It looks like a ruined egg garnish.

843
00:33:15,626 --> 00:33:16,761
It looks too thick.

844
00:33:16,828 --> 00:33:18,730
-It's the egg garnish I ruined.
-Shush.

845
00:33:18,796 --> 00:33:20,064
Is he making egg garnish?

846
00:33:20,131 --> 00:33:21,766
SHH, THE CRÊPE MAY HEAR YOU

847
00:33:21,833 --> 00:33:24,936
-It's tough to control the heat.
-It's tough to control the heat.

848
00:33:25,003 --> 00:33:26,671
Let me try this tomato sauce pasta.

849
00:33:26,738 --> 00:33:29,073
-Pasta.
-Let me try this one.

850
00:33:29,140 --> 00:33:32,610
Will Parfait Princess be ready
for the grand opening?

851
00:33:32,677 --> 00:33:35,546
The pasta is a bit undercooked.

852
00:33:35,613 --> 00:33:36,881
-Add water.
-Come on.

853
00:33:36,948 --> 00:33:38,316
-It's hard.
-I have time.

854
00:33:38,383 --> 00:33:40,351
-Four minutes.
-It's still too hard.

855
00:33:40,418 --> 00:33:42,920
You'll need to work on the pasta.

856
00:33:42,987 --> 00:33:45,189
What's this for?

857
00:33:45,256 --> 00:33:46,457
It's tomato soup.

858
00:33:46,524 --> 00:33:48,126
-Tomato soup.
-In Hong Kong style.

859
00:33:48,192 --> 00:33:49,427
-The soup.
-The soup.

860
00:33:49,494 --> 00:33:51,896
Does the soup taste like
it's for a grown woman?

861
00:33:51,963 --> 00:33:53,097
Or is it for an old dude?

862
00:33:53,164 --> 00:33:55,733
-It's for an old dude.
-It is?

863
00:33:55,800 --> 00:33:58,136
-It's way too spicy.
-It's too spicy?

864
00:33:58,202 --> 00:34:00,805
Pretty, grown women love spicy food.

865
00:34:00,872 --> 00:34:02,907
-Pretty…
-Look at me. I love spicy food.

866
00:34:03,141 --> 00:34:04,475
DOESN'T MEAN THE SAME THING, X

867
00:34:04,542 --> 00:34:06,411
I love spicy food.

868
00:34:06,477 --> 00:34:09,213
I wonder how Jung-an will like it.

869
00:34:09,280 --> 00:34:10,681
Add some butter.

870
00:34:10,748 --> 00:34:12,617
Wait, where's butter?

871
00:34:12,917 --> 00:34:14,585
MUST'VE BEEN SPICY FOR HER TOO

872
00:34:14,652 --> 00:34:17,355
Bring down the spiciness,
and it'll be much better.

873
00:34:17,422 --> 00:34:18,890
Two and a half minutes left.

874
00:34:18,956 --> 00:34:20,691
There's no time.

875
00:34:20,758 --> 00:34:22,260
What will they do?

876
00:34:22,326 --> 00:34:25,563
Jong-won looks too busy.
He's facing a crisis.

877
00:34:25,630 --> 00:34:28,399
-What's that? <i>Nurungji.</i>
-What's that for?

878
00:34:28,599 --> 00:34:31,869
JUNG-AN'S FAVORITE NURUNGJI SNACK

879
00:34:31,936 --> 00:34:34,005
Jong-won looks busy,

880
00:34:34,072 --> 00:34:35,173
so I won't mess with him.

881
00:34:35,973 --> 00:34:37,375
CHUCKLE…

882
00:34:37,442 --> 00:34:38,609
I have too much to do.

883
00:34:38,676 --> 00:34:40,411
-He has too much to do.
-Oh, no.

884
00:34:40,478 --> 00:34:41,879
-Sung-ju.
-Yes?

885
00:34:41,946 --> 00:34:42,980
-Sung-ju.
-Yes?

886
00:34:43,047 --> 00:34:44,582
Can you mess with Jong-won?

887
00:34:44,782 --> 00:34:46,217
TEEHEE…

888
00:34:46,284 --> 00:34:48,186
-If I did…
-Wait, no.

889
00:34:48,252 --> 00:34:49,587
…he might throw something at me.

890
00:34:49,787 --> 00:34:50,621
GETTING UP

891
00:34:50,688 --> 00:34:51,989
Something smells burning.

892
00:34:52,223 --> 00:34:53,191
A BURNING SMELL?

893
00:34:53,257 --> 00:34:55,793
-What's burning?
-This pasta almost got burnt.

894
00:34:55,860 --> 00:34:57,962
-It's not burnt.
-He's in a mess.

895
00:34:58,029 --> 00:34:59,297
Only two minutes left.

896
00:34:59,997 --> 00:35:00,932
SPEEDING UP!

897
00:35:01,165 --> 00:35:02,100
LEMON ZEST

898
00:35:02,600 --> 00:35:03,835
OLIVE OIL

899
00:35:03,901 --> 00:35:05,536
-A minute and a half.
-Piping bag.

900
00:35:05,603 --> 00:35:07,905
-A minute?
-Nam-no, put this in too.

901
00:35:08,372 --> 00:35:10,842
FILLING 6: CUCUMBER HUMMUS

902
00:35:11,075 --> 00:35:12,276
THE GROWN MAN STARTS TO FEEL RUSHED

903
00:35:12,577 --> 00:35:14,579
FLIPPING THE CRÊPE

904
00:35:14,645 --> 00:35:17,682
-Wait a second.
-It's <i>nurungji,</i> so it's fine.

905
00:35:18,282 --> 00:35:19,750
PULL OUT THE CRÊPE

906
00:35:19,817 --> 00:35:20,852
Start layering now.

907
00:35:20,918 --> 00:35:22,787
The business might get shut down.

908
00:35:23,287 --> 00:35:24,288
ROLLING WITH A CHOPSTICK

909
00:35:24,355 --> 00:35:25,556
He's rolling it like that?

910
00:35:25,623 --> 00:35:26,524
FILLING 7: CRÊPE

911
00:35:26,591 --> 00:35:28,893
-A minute and 25 seconds.
-There's no time.

912
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:29,794
Gosh.

913
00:35:31,095 --> 00:35:34,599
Everything seems to be
going smoothly for Eun-yeong.

914
00:35:34,665 --> 00:35:37,068
She's much calmer in comparison.

915
00:35:37,135 --> 00:35:40,671
-Yes, a grown woman is calm.
-The grown woman vibe.

916
00:35:40,738 --> 00:35:43,941
-A grown woman would…
-Whoa.

917
00:35:45,610 --> 00:35:47,612
Whew, I almost knocked it off
while running my mouth.

918
00:35:48,012 --> 00:35:49,780
PEOPLE NEVER CHANGE…

919
00:35:51,082 --> 00:35:52,783
The grown woman is very calm.

920
00:35:52,850 --> 00:35:54,318
She seems relaxed.

921
00:35:54,385 --> 00:35:56,654
Chef Eun-yeong remains calm.

922
00:35:56,721 --> 00:36:01,125
She has not won against Jong-won
this year.

923
00:36:02,059 --> 00:36:03,494
One minute.

924
00:36:03,561 --> 00:36:05,730
He's making eggs now?

925
00:36:05,796 --> 00:36:08,599
-But there's a minute left.
-One minute left.

926
00:36:08,666 --> 00:36:10,735
-One minute.
-One minute.

927
00:36:11,035 --> 00:36:12,770
HURRY UP

928
00:36:12,837 --> 00:36:13,804
The time.

929
00:36:14,038 --> 00:36:15,373
FLUSTERED CHUCKLE

930
00:36:15,439 --> 00:36:17,141
Jong-won is in a frenzy.

931
00:36:17,208 --> 00:36:19,443
-The eggs.
-Fifty seconds.

932
00:36:19,510 --> 00:36:23,214
I haven't seen him
look this rushed before.

933
00:36:23,281 --> 00:36:24,448
CUBING THE STEAK!

934
00:36:24,515 --> 00:36:26,951
Eun-yeong will probably win
if her dishes are decent.

935
00:36:27,018 --> 00:36:29,587
I don't think he'll make all 11.

936
00:36:29,654 --> 00:36:31,355
Jong-won faces a crisis.

937
00:36:32,223 --> 00:36:33,057
Thirty seconds.

938
00:36:33,124 --> 00:36:34,025
CLANK

939
00:36:34,592 --> 00:36:36,460
Jong-won looks anxious.

940
00:36:36,527 --> 00:36:38,296
It's a huge emergency.

941
00:36:38,362 --> 00:36:39,597
The good vibes core in a frenzy.

942
00:36:39,664 --> 00:36:41,799
-Jung-an, don't worry.
-Thirty seconds.

943
00:36:41,866 --> 00:36:43,534
No, that's The Gentleman Sucks.

944
00:36:43,601 --> 00:36:45,403
-The Gentleman Sucks?
-The Gentleman Sucks.

945
00:36:45,469 --> 00:36:47,471
Be quiet!

946
00:36:47,538 --> 00:36:48,940
Is that The Gentleman Sucks?

947
00:36:49,006 --> 00:36:52,109
The Gentleman Sucks is making a comeback!

948
00:36:52,176 --> 00:36:53,678
It's finally here again!

949
00:36:53,744 --> 00:36:55,780
It's remade by Jong-won!

950
00:36:55,846 --> 00:36:57,782
-It's an emergency.
-It's a total emergency.

951
00:36:58,216 --> 00:37:00,184
6. FLATBREAD

952
00:37:00,851 --> 00:37:01,886
Ten seconds.

953
00:37:01,953 --> 00:37:03,421
You have to finish up.

954
00:37:04,355 --> 00:37:06,057
Eun-yeong definitely has an advantage.

955
00:37:06,123 --> 00:37:07,959
-Four. Three.
-Three.

956
00:37:08,025 --> 00:37:09,961
-Two. One.
-Two. One.

957
00:37:10,027 --> 00:37:12,196
-Four. Three.
-Three.

958
00:37:12,263 --> 00:37:13,864
EUN-YEONG IS FINISHED!

959
00:37:13,931 --> 00:37:16,400
-That's it.
-Time's up.

960
00:37:16,467 --> 00:37:19,303
-I'll just plate it.
-You can't make new dishes.

961
00:37:19,370 --> 00:37:21,505
Just let me plate it.
I have 11 ingredients.

962
00:37:21,572 --> 00:37:24,375
You're quite relatable today, Jong-won.

963
00:37:24,442 --> 00:37:25,776
I've never seen him like that.

964
00:37:25,843 --> 00:37:28,179
-You were like a grown woman.
-Right?

965
00:37:28,246 --> 00:37:30,147
-The Han Yu-ju among chefs.
-So chic.

966
00:37:30,214 --> 00:37:31,882
She was relaxed and unshaken.

967
00:37:31,949 --> 00:37:33,884
Never rushed.

968
00:37:34,051 --> 00:37:35,653
BEAUTY IS MY WEAPON

969
00:37:35,720 --> 00:37:37,221
She does not look rushed.

970
00:37:37,655 --> 00:37:38,990
SUBMITS HER DISH ELEGANTLY

971
00:37:39,056 --> 00:37:41,792
-The Chae Jung-an among chefs.
-Should I ring it or not?

972
00:37:41,859 --> 00:37:43,427
-Ring it?
-Please ring the bell.

973
00:37:43,494 --> 00:37:44,528
She rings the bell.

974
00:37:44,762 --> 00:37:46,497
THE GROWN WOMAN IS DONE

975
00:37:46,564 --> 00:37:48,499
He's only allowed to plate the dish.

976
00:37:49,100 --> 00:37:50,968
How's she going to eat it?

977
00:37:51,035 --> 00:37:53,037
STILL AT THE 7TH LAYER

978
00:37:53,104 --> 00:37:55,306
Can I eat that? Is it okay to eat it?

979
00:37:55,373 --> 00:37:57,541
She has to suck it all the way down
to get the meat.

980
00:37:57,608 --> 00:37:59,844
-I have to use a straw?
-I'm sorry.

981
00:37:59,910 --> 00:38:02,713
Yu-ju from 2007 could probably suck it in,

982
00:38:02,780 --> 00:38:05,850
-but I may not have enough power.
-With the breath.

983
00:38:05,916 --> 00:38:07,184
LACK OF LUNG CAPACITY

984
00:38:07,251 --> 00:38:08,419
I'm scared.

985
00:38:08,486 --> 00:38:12,923
-Was this an homage to my dish?
-One layer each.

986
00:38:12,990 --> 00:38:15,326
And now, the final layer.

987
00:38:15,393 --> 00:38:16,994
The 10th, so that's the 11th.

988
00:38:17,561 --> 00:38:18,896
It's like whipped cream.

989
00:38:18,963 --> 00:38:20,498
10. CREAM CHEESE

990
00:38:20,564 --> 00:38:21,899
That's the final touch.

991
00:38:21,966 --> 00:38:24,135
Do you have an umbrella? He does!

992
00:38:24,201 --> 00:38:27,438
-Does he really have one?
-The umbrella is very important.

993
00:38:27,505 --> 00:38:28,973
And there's always a wafer.

994
00:38:29,040 --> 00:38:32,343
Again, let me tell you that he's simply
plating what he has already made.

995
00:38:32,410 --> 00:38:34,812
Jong-won finishes too.

996
00:38:36,113 --> 00:38:37,715
He actually made a parfait.

997
00:38:37,782 --> 00:38:39,917
-It looks like that dessert.
-Yes.

998
00:38:39,984 --> 00:38:42,787
THE DISHES MADE FOR THE GROWN WOMAN

999
00:38:42,853 --> 00:38:44,555
It's in the order
of the appetizer and the main dish.

1000
00:38:44,622 --> 00:38:46,290
The top is the appetizer,
and the bottom is the main dish.

1001
00:38:46,357 --> 00:38:49,193
-I see.
-You can slowly work your way down.

1002
00:38:49,260 --> 00:38:52,963
Everything has been made for Jung-an.

1003
00:38:53,030 --> 00:38:54,899
-Things were quite rushed.
-Yes.

1004
00:38:54,965 --> 00:38:56,901
<i>-You seemed anxious too.</i>
-Oh, no!

1005
00:38:56,967 --> 00:38:59,503
I began to break out in a cold sweat.

1006
00:38:59,570 --> 00:39:01,505
And especially Chef Jong-won.

1007
00:39:01,572 --> 00:39:04,408
-He's never like this.
-Exactly.

1008
00:39:04,475 --> 00:39:07,345
-But he must've wanted to try harder…
-I could feel it.

1009
00:39:07,411 --> 00:39:10,781
…so he needed
a little more time to finish at the end.

1010
00:39:10,848 --> 00:39:14,385
But the energy he showed was so great,

1011
00:39:14,452 --> 00:39:16,287
-and I'm quite excited.
-I see.

1012
00:39:16,354 --> 00:39:17,755
The Han Yu-ju among chefs,

1013
00:39:17,822 --> 00:39:20,224
Chef Eun-yeong finished her dish first.

1014
00:39:21,726 --> 00:39:23,027
That smile keeps lingering in my mind.

1015
00:39:23,094 --> 00:39:26,931
The Han Yu-ju among chefs,
please try Chef Eun-yeong's food first.

1016
00:39:27,498 --> 00:39:30,501
A GROWN WOMAN'S BRUNCH

1017
00:39:31,302 --> 00:39:34,071
A CHINESE-STYLE BRUNCH AT HANNAM-DONG

1018
00:39:35,005 --> 00:39:37,241
A DASH OF ADULT SPICINESS

1019
00:39:37,308 --> 00:39:40,311
First, try the avocado and burrata.

1020
00:39:40,378 --> 00:39:42,413
Next, the shrimp toast and the soup,

1021
00:39:42,480 --> 00:39:44,782
and try that one last.

1022
00:39:45,182 --> 00:39:46,951
A HEARTY MEAL

1023
00:39:47,017 --> 00:39:48,519
Should I eat it like Yu-ju?

1024
00:39:48,586 --> 00:39:51,589
-You have to eat it like Yu-ju.
-Okay.

1025
00:39:52,757 --> 00:39:55,192
HAN YU-JU AT HANNAM-DONG

1026
00:39:57,762 --> 00:39:59,964
The atmosphere just changed.

1027
00:40:00,030 --> 00:40:01,632
Her eyes have changed.

1028
00:40:03,334 --> 00:40:04,502
MMM

1029
00:40:04,568 --> 00:40:05,836
What's it like?

1030
00:40:05,903 --> 00:40:07,171
It's working up my appetite.

1031
00:40:07,238 --> 00:40:09,840
-"It's working up my appetite."
-It really does.

1032
00:40:09,907 --> 00:40:12,343
That's how Yu-ju would say it.
"It's working up my appetite."

1033
00:40:12,410 --> 00:40:13,611
She's just like her.

1034
00:40:13,677 --> 00:40:16,046
Her line sounded more like Madam Han.

1035
00:40:16,113 --> 00:40:17,581
"It's working up my appetite."

1036
00:40:17,782 --> 00:40:20,384
THE TRACE OF TIME SHOWS IN HER LANGUAGE

1037
00:40:21,252 --> 00:40:24,221
It's a little bit saltier with the sauce.

1038
00:40:24,288 --> 00:40:27,391
-Yes, it's a good appetizer.
-I should mix it with the sauce.

1039
00:40:27,458 --> 00:40:31,228
It's already reminding me of white wine.

1040
00:40:31,295 --> 00:40:32,663
A THUMBS-UP

1041
00:40:32,730 --> 00:40:35,633
She's already giving a thumbs-up
after the appetizer.

1042
00:40:35,699 --> 00:40:37,868
-The shrimp toast?
-Looks good.

1043
00:40:37,935 --> 00:40:39,737
She loves shrimp toast.

1044
00:40:42,706 --> 00:40:45,009
No sauce for this one?

1045
00:40:45,075 --> 00:40:48,145
No, but please eat it with arugula.

1046
00:40:48,212 --> 00:40:50,080
The shrimp looks so plump.

1047
00:40:50,147 --> 00:40:51,315
FALLING OVER

1048
00:40:51,382 --> 00:40:53,717
-Was it burnt?
-It's so good.

1049
00:40:53,784 --> 00:40:55,920
"It's so good."

1050
00:40:56,754 --> 00:41:00,591
It's such a wonder
that I had these ingredients in my fridge.

1051
00:41:01,158 --> 00:41:02,860
PICKING UP ARUGULA IN EXCITEMENT

1052
00:41:02,927 --> 00:41:05,796
In the end,
it's all about what you make with it.

1053
00:41:05,863 --> 00:41:07,398
-And how.
-Yes.

1054
00:41:07,631 --> 00:41:08,799
I WANT A BITE…

1055
00:41:08,866 --> 00:41:12,470
She's crazy for shrimp toasts.

1056
00:41:12,536 --> 00:41:14,772
She's enjoying it very excitedly.

1057
00:41:14,839 --> 00:41:16,740
The moment I took a bite of this,

1058
00:41:16,807 --> 00:41:19,610
it was so good that
it suddenly gave me better eyesight.

1059
00:41:19,677 --> 00:41:22,213
-That happens.
-I suddenly feel so awake.

1060
00:41:22,279 --> 00:41:25,182
-Next, the tomato soup?
-Tomato soup.

1061
00:41:25,249 --> 00:41:27,985
-It's tomato soup in Hong Kong style.
-Tomato soup in Hong Kong style.

1062
00:41:28,052 --> 00:41:29,920
It's a great hangover cure, right?

1063
00:41:29,987 --> 00:41:30,921
You're right.

1064
00:41:31,722 --> 00:41:34,158
-Tomato soup in Hong Kong style.
-There's octopus.

1065
00:41:34,225 --> 00:41:35,893
Yes, it's made with octopus.

1066
00:41:37,094 --> 00:41:39,964
BLOWING ON IT FOR A CAREFUL BITE

1067
00:41:42,199 --> 00:41:43,033
EYES SHUT

1068
00:41:43,100 --> 00:41:44,702
It must be hot.

1069
00:41:44,768 --> 00:41:46,770
-It's spicy.
-It's spicy.

1070
00:41:46,837 --> 00:41:48,105
-Is it spicy?
-Is it?

1071
00:41:48,172 --> 00:41:49,940
-See? I told you.
-It's a bit spicy.

1072
00:41:50,808 --> 00:41:51,976
AHEM

1073
00:41:52,042 --> 00:41:52,910
"Ahem."

1074
00:41:52,977 --> 00:41:55,045
That was Yu-ju's response. "Ahem."

1075
00:41:55,112 --> 00:41:56,213
A REACTION ON ANOTHER LEVEL

1076
00:41:56,847 --> 00:41:58,482
But I'm a grown woman.

1077
00:41:58,816 --> 00:42:01,185
IT'S NOT EASY TO BE A GROWN-UP

1078
00:42:01,252 --> 00:42:04,188
-I want to dip that toast in this.
-Right.

1079
00:42:04,255 --> 00:42:05,089
It's so rich.

1080
00:42:05,155 --> 00:42:06,757
-It's rich?
-Yes.

1081
00:42:06,824 --> 00:42:08,592
-Isn't it too spicy?
-Very potent.

1082
00:42:08,659 --> 00:42:11,896
If you aren't ready, the first bite

1083
00:42:11,962 --> 00:42:13,898
can punch you in the face.

1084
00:42:13,964 --> 00:42:16,200
-Punch in the face? You're Yu-ju.
-It hits you.

1085
00:42:16,267 --> 00:42:18,569
-Yu-ju can't say that.
-You're Yu-ju right now.

1086
00:42:18,636 --> 00:42:21,305
-It must've been strong.
-It suddenly hits you.

1087
00:42:21,372 --> 00:42:25,175
And then, your senses
slowly get used to it and enjoy it.

1088
00:42:25,242 --> 00:42:29,146
-Please stay as Yu-ju for this one.
-Yes, remember, Han Yu-ju core.

1089
00:42:29,213 --> 00:42:33,150
Next, grilled pork neck.

1090
00:42:33,217 --> 00:42:35,786
It's <i>chashu</i> in Hong Kong style.

1091
00:42:35,853 --> 00:42:37,154
<i>Chashu</i> in Hong Kong style.

1092
00:42:37,221 --> 00:42:38,956
-Is this just cucumber?
-No.

1093
00:42:39,023 --> 00:42:41,559
<i>I mixed it with condiments</i>
<i>and peanut butter.</i>

1094
00:42:42,560 --> 00:42:44,995
Eun-yeong's good at using peanut butter.

1095
00:42:45,062 --> 00:42:47,231
Cucumber goes very well
with peanut butter.

1096
00:42:48,232 --> 00:42:50,267
THE FROWN OF TRUTH

1097
00:42:50,334 --> 00:42:51,402
NODDING

1098
00:42:51,468 --> 00:42:52,836
-Is it good?
-Yes.

1099
00:42:52,903 --> 00:42:57,908
It brings out the texture of pork neck,

1100
00:42:57,975 --> 00:43:02,279
and the cucumber adds
a nice and refreshing touch.

1101
00:43:02,346 --> 00:43:04,448
-I'm glad she used cucumbers.
-Yes.

1102
00:43:04,515 --> 00:43:07,151
And if I eat it with the soup,

1103
00:43:07,217 --> 00:43:08,786
it'll balance each other out.

1104
00:43:08,852 --> 00:43:12,056
Just like how it's delicious
to eat meat with <i>ssamjang,</i>

1105
00:43:12,122 --> 00:43:15,459
-this soup adds a good balance.
-I see.

1106
00:43:15,526 --> 00:43:18,062
You said you could tell
someone's character by their food.

1107
00:43:18,128 --> 00:43:20,931
-What's her character like?
-Can you sense it?

1108
00:43:20,998 --> 00:43:23,634
If I were to judge the chef by this food,

1109
00:43:23,701 --> 00:43:26,136
I'd say it's a thoughtful and wise

1110
00:43:26,203 --> 00:43:27,271
old dude.

1111
00:43:27,338 --> 00:43:30,107
-Old dude.
-I'm just joking.

1112
00:43:30,174 --> 00:43:31,742
-Old dude.
-I meant a young lady.

1113
00:43:31,809 --> 00:43:33,611
She touches her chin.

1114
00:43:33,677 --> 00:43:35,713
-Has it grown?
-Not yet.

1115
00:43:35,779 --> 00:43:37,348
Does it grow by the evening?

1116
00:43:38,916 --> 00:43:41,318
But that was how I felt.

1117
00:43:41,385 --> 00:43:42,886
Next, the dish in question.

1118
00:43:42,953 --> 00:43:45,823
-Parfait Princess.
-What's he going to do?

1119
00:43:45,889 --> 00:43:48,525
STEAK, PASTA, SCRAMBLED EGGS, CORN,
CUCUMBER HUMMUS, FLATBREAD

1120
00:43:48,592 --> 00:43:51,228
AVOCADO PURÉE, FIG, ARUGULA,
CREAM CHEESE, CRÊPE

1121
00:43:52,229 --> 00:43:55,165
THERE HAS NEVER BEEN A PARFAIT LIKE THIS

1122
00:43:55,799 --> 00:43:59,370
CHEF SON JONG-WON'S PARFAIT PRINCESS

1123
00:43:59,436 --> 00:44:01,939
Can you tell us about the layers?

1124
00:44:02,006 --> 00:44:04,308
I had prepared a dessert,
so may I serve that too?

1125
00:44:04,375 --> 00:44:06,410
-Dessert?
-You made dessert?

1126
00:44:06,844 --> 00:44:09,613
THAT'S HOW THOUGHTFUL
THE GOOD VIBES GROWN MAN IS

1127
00:44:09,680 --> 00:44:11,915
I prepared a dessert
perfect for a grown woman.

1128
00:44:11,982 --> 00:44:14,284
-White wine.
-White wine.

1129
00:44:14,351 --> 00:44:17,554
White wine on the rocks.

1130
00:44:17,621 --> 00:44:18,555
It's nice in summer.

1131
00:44:18,622 --> 00:44:21,892
Many people may find this odd,

1132
00:44:21,959 --> 00:44:24,428
but Yu-ju, the grown woman, doesn't care.

1133
00:44:24,495 --> 00:44:26,196
-She's a grown woman.
-She doesn't care.

1134
00:44:26,263 --> 00:44:28,599
It'll taste so good
with a dash of that soju.

1135
00:44:28,966 --> 00:44:31,101
MISSING CHAIRMAN JOO'S SOJU
FROM THE PREVIOUS EPISODE

1136
00:44:31,168 --> 00:44:33,871
She wants to mix this with Sang-uk's soju.

1137
00:44:33,937 --> 00:44:36,273
-That's Yu-ju, the grown woman, for you.
-Right?

1138
00:44:36,340 --> 00:44:38,676
I realized after watching Eun-yeong

1139
00:44:38,742 --> 00:44:40,778
that she actually seemed to think
she was Han Yu-ju,

1140
00:44:40,844 --> 00:44:43,313
so she made the food she likes.

1141
00:44:43,380 --> 00:44:46,216
But back then, Hannam-dong wasn't popular.

1142
00:44:46,283 --> 00:44:48,485
It was Gangnam Station,
right behind Tower Records.

1143
00:44:49,453 --> 00:44:50,287
-Yes.
-He knows.

1144
00:44:50,354 --> 00:44:52,256
-That's where you eat parfaits.
-That's true.

1145
00:44:52,322 --> 00:44:54,758
So I imagined Yu-ju

1146
00:44:54,825 --> 00:44:58,128
waiting for someone at a café
behind Tower Records,

1147
00:44:58,195 --> 00:45:00,898
-eating parfait while sweeping her hair.
-Odyssey Nightclub.

1148
00:45:00,964 --> 00:45:02,566
Is that accurate?

1149
00:45:02,633 --> 00:45:04,168
At that time,

1150
00:45:04,234 --> 00:45:06,704
-I lived north of the river.
-She was north of the river.

1151
00:45:06,770 --> 00:45:08,238
Then it's Hannam-dong.

1152
00:45:08,539 --> 00:45:10,174
A MISTAKE IN THE SETTING

1153
00:45:10,240 --> 00:45:12,242
-She was in the north.
-She hung in the north,

1154
00:45:12,309 --> 00:45:14,645
so she doesn't know Gangnam too well.

1155
00:45:14,712 --> 00:45:16,513
How should she eat this? From the top?

1156
00:45:16,580 --> 00:45:19,717
You'll see cream cheese, fig, and arugula.

1157
00:45:19,783 --> 00:45:21,885
Scoop them up with a spoon and have some.

1158
00:45:21,952 --> 00:45:22,886
From the top.

1159
00:45:22,953 --> 00:45:26,223
Let me serve you just the first bite.

1160
00:45:27,024 --> 00:45:30,928
You should eat the layers
in the right order.

1161
00:45:30,994 --> 00:45:33,764
-He topped that with <i>nurungji.</i>
-This feels odd.

1162
00:45:33,831 --> 00:45:36,200
I feel like it should be cold,
but it's warm to the touch.

1163
00:45:36,266 --> 00:45:39,403
-It's weird. It's warm.
-You're right.

1164
00:45:40,070 --> 00:45:41,171
I wonder what it's like.

1165
00:45:41,238 --> 00:45:43,173
A SPOONFUL OF CREAM CHEESE AND FIG

1166
00:45:43,540 --> 00:45:45,509
A BIG BITE

1167
00:45:46,376 --> 00:45:49,747
-That's a dish I can't handle.
-This one?

1168
00:45:49,813 --> 00:45:53,784
-Because you don't like cheese.
-Yes, all that cheese…

1169
00:45:54,585 --> 00:45:57,287
At times, I eat a big lump of this.

1170
00:45:57,354 --> 00:45:59,890
-The cheese?
-Yes, I love it so much.

1171
00:46:00,257 --> 00:46:03,360
THIS TIME, WITH ARUGULA

1172
00:46:03,927 --> 00:46:06,764
It tastes familiar and comfortable.

1173
00:46:06,830 --> 00:46:07,965
-That's great.
-Something I like.

1174
00:46:08,031 --> 00:46:11,635
Next, try the avocado and flatbread.

1175
00:46:12,836 --> 00:46:14,805
She's waiting for someone.

1176
00:46:14,872 --> 00:46:17,574
-I should. I'm alone right now.
-You are.

1177
00:46:17,641 --> 00:46:19,777
You're alone at the moment.

1178
00:46:19,843 --> 00:46:21,245
When will they come?

1179
00:46:22,312 --> 00:46:23,280
I LIKE THIS DRAMA

1180
00:46:23,347 --> 00:46:24,481
That's the vibe.

1181
00:46:24,548 --> 00:46:26,416
You've paged your party already.

1182
00:46:26,483 --> 00:46:28,485
-"Come quickly, my angel."
-Right, angel.

1183
00:46:29,419 --> 00:46:31,922
A BITE WITH A STRAIGHT FACE

1184
00:46:31,989 --> 00:46:33,157
That's Yu-ju's vibe.

1185
00:46:33,657 --> 00:46:36,627
WILL THE GIRL FROM THE NORTH OF THE RIVER
FALL INTO GANGNAM'S CHARMS?

1186
00:46:36,693 --> 00:46:38,695
I suddenly chewed on corn,

1187
00:46:38,762 --> 00:46:41,231
something I didn't expect to eat.

1188
00:46:41,298 --> 00:46:43,801
<i>-He had steamed them, right?</i>
<i>-Yes.</i>

1189
00:46:43,867 --> 00:46:45,402
She can taste corn too.

1190
00:46:45,469 --> 00:46:49,840
I feel like a girl
from the north of the river at Gangnam,

1191
00:46:49,907 --> 00:46:51,875
feeling awkward but acting like I'm not.

1192
00:46:51,942 --> 00:46:53,877
You feel awkward, but you're pretending.

1193
00:46:54,444 --> 00:46:56,213
"Do girls in Gangnam eat this?"

1194
00:46:57,014 --> 00:46:59,650
-But I'm from across the river.
-Pretending like you're from Gangnam.

1195
00:46:59,716 --> 00:47:03,587
-Pretending as if I've always eaten this.
-As if it's your usual dish.

1196
00:47:03,654 --> 00:47:05,189
That pasta is the problem.

1197
00:47:05,255 --> 00:47:07,191
Isn't there steak underneath the pasta?

1198
00:47:07,257 --> 00:47:08,959
-She has a lot to work on.
-She does.

1199
00:47:09,026 --> 00:47:12,196
-She has to eat it in order.
-There's the pasta!

1200
00:47:12,262 --> 00:47:14,364
-She pulled out a strand of pasta!
-The pasta.

1201
00:47:14,431 --> 00:47:15,499
There's the pasta.

1202
00:47:15,566 --> 00:47:17,601
-There's the pasta now.
-She pulled it out.

1203
00:47:17,668 --> 00:47:20,470
I wouldn't feel lonely
even as I eat this alone.

1204
00:47:20,537 --> 00:47:21,972
It's not boring.

1205
00:47:22,039 --> 00:47:23,240
And it's steaming.

1206
00:47:23,307 --> 00:47:25,576
-It's still hot.
-I keep meeting new friends.

1207
00:47:25,642 --> 00:47:27,978
I can't believe it.
That parfait is steaming.

1208
00:47:28,045 --> 00:47:32,649
It's an homage to my dish.
It looks more like mine.

1209
00:47:32,716 --> 00:47:35,352
-A shocking dish made by Jong-won.
-What's it like?

1210
00:47:35,419 --> 00:47:37,187
What's it like?

1211
00:47:37,754 --> 00:47:40,691
The pasta brought
many other friends with it.

1212
00:47:41,692 --> 00:47:44,595
It had to pass through the sauces.

1213
00:47:45,028 --> 00:47:47,598
THE TASTE OF A HEARTRENDING FRIENDSHIP(?)

1214
00:47:47,664 --> 00:47:49,800
<i>-It met this and that.</i>
<i>-Yes.</i>

1215
00:47:49,867 --> 00:47:51,835
<i>-What a playboy.</i>
<i>-Playboy?</i>

1216
00:47:51,902 --> 00:47:54,404
That pasta must be popular.

1217
00:47:54,471 --> 00:47:56,473
-It's like that.
-Well, that's the pasta.

1218
00:47:56,540 --> 00:47:59,543
-But what about the steak?
-That's another problem.

1219
00:47:59,810 --> 00:48:01,712
HAS A LONG WAY TO GO TO REACH THE STEAK

1220
00:48:01,778 --> 00:48:03,447
Do we have long chopsticks?

1221
00:48:03,513 --> 00:48:05,782
-We do.
-One second. Let me help.

1222
00:48:05,849 --> 00:48:08,852
-She got it.
-There it is. There's the meat.

1223
00:48:08,919 --> 00:48:11,088
Does it feel like this when you fish?

1224
00:48:11,321 --> 00:48:12,756
A NEW KIND OF FISHING

1225
00:48:12,823 --> 00:48:14,758
-It's a big catch!
-You got it!

1226
00:48:14,825 --> 00:48:17,494
-You caught it.
-I got this.

1227
00:48:17,561 --> 00:48:19,830
-It's there.
-Whoa.

1228
00:48:20,097 --> 00:48:22,165
THE MEAT KEEPS RESISTING

1229
00:48:22,232 --> 00:48:25,335
-We need a scoop net.
-Where's the scoop net?

1230
00:48:25,402 --> 00:48:27,004
Where's the scoop net?

1231
00:48:27,070 --> 00:48:29,273
What was the bait?

1232
00:48:29,339 --> 00:48:30,941
This one also brought friends.

1233
00:48:31,441 --> 00:48:33,744
Even a crane
won't be able to reach that deep.

1234
00:48:34,444 --> 00:48:37,514
BUT THIS GROWN WOMAN
GOT THE MEAT SUCCESSFULLY

1235
00:48:37,881 --> 00:48:39,182
GETTING DIZZY

1236
00:48:39,483 --> 00:48:41,685
CHUCKLING

1237
00:48:41,952 --> 00:48:44,187
DID THE STEAK MAKE HER SMILE?

1238
00:48:44,254 --> 00:48:47,257
Near the border of Seocho-dong,

1239
00:48:47,324 --> 00:48:49,626
a small business owner
who went through many failures

1240
00:48:49,693 --> 00:48:52,262
wanted to get a new start.

1241
00:48:52,329 --> 00:48:55,265
That was why he hired
a chef who studied abroad.

1242
00:48:55,332 --> 00:48:58,068
-An experimental dish.
-That's how he made this one.

1243
00:48:58,135 --> 00:49:00,804
-But the ingredients are still great.
-Banpo style.

1244
00:49:01,204 --> 00:49:03,807
LASTLY, FINISH ELEGANTLY WITH WHITE WINE

1245
00:49:03,874 --> 00:49:05,976
She's full of charms.

1246
00:49:06,410 --> 00:49:08,478
INSANELY CHARMING

1247
00:49:08,545 --> 00:49:10,213
That was too experimental.

1248
00:49:10,280 --> 00:49:12,416
You didn't add soju in here,
but it tastes like it.

1249
00:49:14,284 --> 00:49:15,852
I know what this taste is.

1250
00:49:15,919 --> 00:49:17,888
A SERIES OF UNCANNY EVENTS

1251
00:49:17,955 --> 00:49:20,624
What's the character of this chef like?

1252
00:49:21,191 --> 00:49:23,727
-I wonder what it's like.
-Should I say it now?

1253
00:49:23,794 --> 00:49:25,862
I'm not always like this.

1254
00:49:26,263 --> 00:49:28,832
AN URGENT EXCUSE(?)

1255
00:49:28,899 --> 00:49:30,300
-Do you feel anxious?
-It's true.

1256
00:49:30,367 --> 00:49:33,136
-We want to know what he's really like.
-What he's like on the inside.

1257
00:49:33,704 --> 00:49:35,339
He has a bit of greed in his heart.

1258
00:49:35,405 --> 00:49:37,607
-Greed?
-Greed? What kind of greed?

1259
00:49:37,674 --> 00:49:39,943
-She sees greed.
-After succeeding many times,

1260
00:49:40,010 --> 00:49:42,279
those people who are in first place

1261
00:49:42,346 --> 00:49:45,148
try to beat themselves to stay at the top.

1262
00:49:45,215 --> 00:49:46,116
A fight against himself.

1263
00:49:46,183 --> 00:49:48,485
-Yes.
-Trying to break the limit.

1264
00:49:48,552 --> 00:49:51,989
Then clueless people like me show up,
take pictures,

1265
00:49:52,055 --> 00:49:54,257
and act like I get it.

1266
00:49:54,324 --> 00:49:56,760
-The top chef forces me to think that way.
-You're forced?

1267
00:49:57,127 --> 00:49:59,496
THE FOREVER FIRST PLACE'S
PARFAIT GASLIGHTING

1268
00:49:59,563 --> 00:50:00,964
All right, then.

1269
00:50:01,031 --> 00:50:03,600
Sang-uk should try some.

1270
00:50:03,667 --> 00:50:05,602
Let me try just the soup.

1271
00:50:07,371 --> 00:50:10,841
A BITE OF EUN-YEONG'S TOMATO SOUP FIRST

1272
00:50:11,441 --> 00:50:12,876
-Gosh.
-Isn't it spicy?

1273
00:50:12,943 --> 00:50:14,678
Isn't it? See? I told you so.

1274
00:50:14,745 --> 00:50:16,480
You can take spicy food, right?

1275
00:50:16,546 --> 00:50:19,516
-I love spicy food.
-See? He eats it well.

1276
00:50:20,250 --> 00:50:21,084
WET EYES(?)

1277
00:50:21,151 --> 00:50:22,853
He keeps blinking.

1278
00:50:24,421 --> 00:50:28,158
-I love this. It's perfectly spicy.
-Do you like it?

1279
00:50:29,092 --> 00:50:31,895
You won't be able to eat this
if you don't love spicy food.

1280
00:50:31,962 --> 00:50:33,764
-I see.
-It's quite spicy.

1281
00:50:33,830 --> 00:50:35,665
NEXT, HONG KONG STYLE CHASHU

1282
00:50:35,732 --> 00:50:38,101
-That's not a dip.
-It's not?

1283
00:50:38,168 --> 00:50:40,370
-Eat it with cucumber.
-Right.

1284
00:50:40,437 --> 00:50:42,439
OH, MY, MY

1285
00:50:42,506 --> 00:50:43,774
He loves spicy food.

1286
00:50:43,840 --> 00:50:45,575
Did it look like <i>ssamjang?</i>

1287
00:50:46,610 --> 00:50:47,611
<i>-Ssamjang?</i>
<i>-Ssamjang?</i>

1288
00:50:47,677 --> 00:50:49,646
My hand moved automatically.

1289
00:50:49,946 --> 00:50:52,215
CHAIRMAN JOO CAN BE SO SILLY

1290
00:50:52,282 --> 00:50:55,218
Chairman Joo is just like us.

1291
00:50:55,285 --> 00:50:57,821
-He thought it was <i>ssamjang.</i>
-It just went in there.

1292
00:50:57,888 --> 00:50:59,322
That'll taste good too.

1293
00:50:59,689 --> 00:51:01,091
YUM

1294
00:51:01,158 --> 00:51:02,526
It looks like a raw fish fillet.

1295
00:51:05,195 --> 00:51:06,763
-This is good.
-Isn't it?

1296
00:51:06,830 --> 00:51:09,633
-I find it delicious.
-You do?

1297
00:51:10,467 --> 00:51:14,037
-It's spicy, but…
-He must like that soup.

1298
00:51:14,104 --> 00:51:14,971
Isn't it good?

1299
00:51:15,038 --> 00:51:15,972
HISS…

1300
00:51:16,039 --> 00:51:18,208
It has its charms.

1301
00:51:18,542 --> 00:51:20,911
IT MUST BE SPICY…

1302
00:51:20,977 --> 00:51:22,913
-Told you it was.
-It must be very spicy.

1303
00:51:22,979 --> 00:51:26,583
-All right. It's time to choose.
-Time to choose.

1304
00:51:26,650 --> 00:51:28,952
<i>Press one for Jong-won</i>

1305
00:51:29,019 --> 00:51:31,154
<i>or two for Eun-yeong.</i>

1306
00:51:31,221 --> 00:51:34,057
…or two for Eun-yeong.

1307
00:51:34,124 --> 00:51:35,992
She's using the umbrella.

1308
00:51:36,059 --> 00:51:37,294
She's scaring me.

1309
00:51:37,861 --> 00:51:39,229
She's scaring me.

1310
00:51:39,296 --> 00:51:41,765
THE CHAE JUNG-AN SHOW NEVER ENDS

1311
00:51:42,099 --> 00:51:44,468
DO NOT LET YOUR GUARD DOWN

1312
00:51:44,534 --> 00:51:47,938
-Which dish will Han Yu-ju choose?
-I'm curious.

1313
00:51:48,004 --> 00:51:50,574
Please make your decision
and press the button.

1314
00:51:50,640 --> 00:51:52,109
One for Jong-won, two for Eun-yeong.

1315
00:51:53,243 --> 00:51:54,911
PUSH

1316
00:51:54,978 --> 00:51:56,279
With an umbrella behind her ear.

1317
00:51:56,346 --> 00:51:59,416
Which chef was chosen by Yu-ju?

1318
00:51:59,483 --> 00:52:01,785
Let's find out the result. Show us.

1319
00:52:03,353 --> 00:52:05,655
Jong-won has the sole lead at the moment,

1320
00:52:05,722 --> 00:52:07,524
and Eun-yeong has seven stars.

1321
00:52:07,591 --> 00:52:10,694
She has not won against him this year.

1322
00:52:10,760 --> 00:52:14,598
Will she successfully channel Yu-ju
and beat Jong-won?

1323
00:52:14,664 --> 00:52:18,068
Or will Jong-won
continue to keep his lead?

1324
00:52:18,135 --> 00:52:21,271
Han Yu-ju, the grown woman,
which chef is it?

1325
00:52:22,172 --> 00:52:25,208
Yu-ju's choice! Who's the winner?

1326
00:52:25,275 --> 00:52:28,145
Yu-ju's choice! Who's the winner?

1327
00:52:29,146 --> 00:52:30,380
PARK EUN-YEONG WINS

1328
00:52:30,447 --> 00:52:31,848
Park Eun-yeong wins!

1329
00:52:31,915 --> 00:52:34,451
Who's the winner?

1330
00:52:34,518 --> 00:52:36,653
A GROWN WOMAN, ULA-LEO WINS

1331
00:52:36,720 --> 00:52:37,754
Eun-yeong!

1332
00:52:37,821 --> 00:52:42,292
This was Eun-yeong's first win
against Jong-won this year.

1333
00:52:42,359 --> 00:52:44,394
-Yes.
-Congrats.

1334
00:52:44,461 --> 00:52:46,129
Was the parfait too hard to eat?

1335
00:52:46,196 --> 00:52:47,831
I guess I'm far from success.

1336
00:52:47,898 --> 00:52:49,833
I haven't made it to Gangnam yet.

1337
00:52:49,900 --> 00:52:52,135
-It's not for you. Not yet.
-No.

1338
00:52:52,202 --> 00:52:54,771
But Chef Jong-won didn't lose anything.

1339
00:52:54,838 --> 00:52:56,973
It was his funniest cook-off yet.

1340
00:52:57,874 --> 00:53:00,110
-Really?
-He was funny while cooking.

1341
00:53:00,177 --> 00:53:04,581
-He's after Poong's buzzworthiness.
-You might even believe I made this.

1342
00:53:04,648 --> 00:53:06,950
If I was able to make you laugh out loud,

1343
00:53:07,017 --> 00:53:08,185
I'm pleased with that.

1344
00:53:08,418 --> 00:53:09,686
I'M PROUD OF YOU, SON JONG-WON

1345
00:53:09,753 --> 00:53:12,022
Han Yu-ju feels like an ex-girlfriend
to us all, doesn't she?

1346
00:53:12,088 --> 00:53:13,590
It's time that I let her go.

1347
00:53:15,091 --> 00:53:16,526
-He'll let me go?
-Let her go?

1348
00:53:16,593 --> 00:53:18,128
-He'll let me go?
-He will.

1349
00:53:18,461 --> 00:53:19,596
SHOCKED

1350
00:53:19,663 --> 00:53:20,830
This was to get away from me?

1351
00:53:22,032 --> 00:53:24,534
-To get away from her?
-To get away. "Eat this and get lost."

1352
00:53:24,601 --> 00:53:26,937
-To get away from her?
-To get away?

1353
00:53:27,003 --> 00:53:29,873
Parfait Princess will close down now.

1354
00:53:29,940 --> 00:53:34,010
Eun-yeong, this was your first victory
against Jong-won this year.

1355
00:53:34,077 --> 00:53:36,980
I put up with the pain in my neck

1356
00:53:37,047 --> 00:53:39,716
to live like Yu-ju, the grown woman,
for the last decade or so.

1357
00:53:40,584 --> 00:53:43,019
-And it was all worth it.
-That's right.

1358
00:53:43,086 --> 00:53:45,255
I had always been curious about your food,

1359
00:53:45,322 --> 00:53:48,558
and thank you so much
for making only the things I love.

1360
00:53:48,625 --> 00:53:51,428
Chef Eun-yeong takes her eighth victory.

1361
00:53:51,494 --> 00:53:52,796
Please put a star on her.

1362
00:53:52,862 --> 00:53:56,466
She beats Jong-won
for the first time this season.

1363
00:53:56,533 --> 00:53:58,802
Eun-yeong gets chosen by Han Yu-ju

1364
00:53:58,868 --> 00:54:01,104
and takes her eighth win. Congratulations!

1365
00:54:03,440 --> 00:54:05,842
THE BREAK AFTER THE FIRST COOK-OFF

1366
00:54:05,909 --> 00:54:07,711
-I enjoyed it, Chef Jong-won.
-I'll make

1367
00:54:07,777 --> 00:54:09,846
a better meal for you
if you come to my restaurant.

1368
00:54:09,913 --> 00:54:11,081
-You were funny.
-I tried to be.

1369
00:54:11,147 --> 00:54:12,749
You were funny.

1370
00:54:12,816 --> 00:54:14,451
THAT'S THE DUTY OF THE SHOW'S COMIC…

1371
00:54:14,517 --> 00:54:18,288
<i>The second round of cook-off</i>
<i>with ingredients from Jung-an's fridge.</i>

1372
00:54:18,355 --> 00:54:21,958
Chef Yoon Nam-no, who's dreaming
of overcoming Poongphobia,

1373
00:54:22,025 --> 00:54:25,695
versus Webtoon Artist Kim Poong,
who's dreaming of taking another star.

1374
00:54:25,762 --> 00:54:28,898
Let me tell you about the relationship
between these two.

1375
00:54:28,965 --> 00:54:31,368
So far,
their individual match record shows…

1376
00:54:32,068 --> 00:54:35,472
YOON NAM-NO VS. KIM POONG

1377
00:54:35,538 --> 00:54:39,809
<i>…five cook-offs over the last two years.</i>

1378
00:54:39,876 --> 00:54:42,545
And Chef Nam-no lost all five rounds.

1379
00:54:42,612 --> 00:54:44,681
You still haven't won?

1380
00:54:44,748 --> 00:54:47,717
-It's been ten months since last time.
-Ten months?

1381
00:54:47,784 --> 00:54:49,219
You've been avoiding him well.

1382
00:54:49,286 --> 00:54:51,821
I've been running from him for ten months.

1383
00:54:51,888 --> 00:54:53,323
-I see.
-But come to think of it,

1384
00:54:53,390 --> 00:54:55,325
I didn't just lose against Poong.

1385
00:54:55,892 --> 00:54:57,394
I lost against everyone.

1386
00:54:58,762 --> 00:55:00,530
Wait, you realized that now?

1387
00:55:00,597 --> 00:55:01,631
Yes.

1388
00:55:02,132 --> 00:55:03,933
Two hours ago.

1389
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:06,736
-It wasn't just against Poong.
-I'm glad.

1390
00:55:06,803 --> 00:55:09,205
I guess realizing that made me wake up.

1391
00:55:09,272 --> 00:55:12,175
So I wanted to volunteer
to fight against the evil.

1392
00:55:13,376 --> 00:55:15,312
But you've been doing well lately

1393
00:55:15,378 --> 00:55:17,213
with two wins in a row.

1394
00:55:17,280 --> 00:55:20,183
Are you scared? He's on a winning streak.

1395
00:55:20,250 --> 00:55:23,019
I just found out
he was on a winning streak.

1396
00:55:23,086 --> 00:55:25,522
You were on a winning streak?

1397
00:55:25,588 --> 00:55:27,590
-Congratulations.
-Congrats.

1398
00:55:27,657 --> 00:55:30,860
-He's scared again.
-I'm so annoyed. This again?

1399
00:55:30,927 --> 00:55:33,897
-Why are you doing this already?
-He's getting on his nerves already.

1400
00:55:33,963 --> 00:55:36,966
Nam-no might end up crying
if he wins today.

1401
00:55:37,033 --> 00:55:38,968
-I really think I will.
-Yes.

1402
00:55:39,035 --> 00:55:40,770
He's not my concern.

1403
00:55:40,837 --> 00:55:42,372
I'll just use him as a cushion

1404
00:55:42,439 --> 00:55:44,808
-and bounce off him.
-What?

1405
00:55:44,874 --> 00:55:47,243
-He'll help me continue to win.
-To get stars.

1406
00:55:47,310 --> 00:55:52,082
This next order is titled,
Eats Like a Kid, Chae Jung-an Core.

1407
00:55:52,148 --> 00:55:54,451
I know exactly what kids like to eat,

1408
00:55:54,517 --> 00:55:57,387
because I also love food
like <i>bunsik, </i>pizza, and hamburgers.

1409
00:55:57,454 --> 00:55:59,456
You look like
a grade school kid from here.

1410
00:56:00,824 --> 00:56:03,560
-A kid who wants to be an athlete.
-I was thinking that too.

1411
00:56:03,626 --> 00:56:05,795
-Between 10 and 40 years old.
-He looks healthy.

1412
00:56:05,862 --> 00:56:07,163
It's his chubby face.

1413
00:56:07,230 --> 00:56:08,832
-How adorable.
-Are you popular

1414
00:56:08,898 --> 00:56:10,433
among elementary students?

1415
00:56:10,500 --> 00:56:13,303
My elementary school fans are rough.

1416
00:56:13,370 --> 00:56:15,638
I once ran into one
at a convenience store,

1417
00:56:15,705 --> 00:56:18,074
and the kid said, "Hey, man. Win a star."

1418
00:56:19,609 --> 00:56:21,177
That's rough.

1419
00:56:21,244 --> 00:56:22,579
And he looked at me like this.

1420
00:56:22,645 --> 00:56:25,315
But it was rather comforting.

1421
00:56:25,382 --> 00:56:27,217
Please think of them.

1422
00:56:27,283 --> 00:56:28,852
I'll do my best to win.

1423
00:56:28,918 --> 00:56:32,155
Poong, you also have things to say
when it comes to grade school fans.

1424
00:56:32,222 --> 00:56:35,158
I'm sure
most elementary school kids in Korea

1425
00:56:35,225 --> 00:56:38,361
have seen my dish before.

1426
00:56:38,428 --> 00:56:41,064
THE KIM POONG CORE DISH

1427
00:56:41,131 --> 00:56:43,933
I hear kids calling me
"dung loach" when I walk past them.

1428
00:56:44,167 --> 00:56:47,303
THE CHILDREN'S LIVELY SUPPORT
FOR THE DUNG LOACH

1429
00:56:47,370 --> 00:56:49,272
I thought my name was Dung Loach.

1430
00:56:49,339 --> 00:56:53,143
That's how much kids love me.

1431
00:56:54,077 --> 00:56:56,079
CHEF NAM-NO'S INGREDIENTS:
POTATO SUJEBI, MUGWORT TTEOK

1432
00:56:56,146 --> 00:56:58,148
BRIOCHE, BRIE, GRANA PADANO,
BURRATA, RIBEYE, MILK, LEMON

1433
00:56:58,214 --> 00:57:00,717
First up, Chef Nam-no.
What are you making?

1434
00:57:00,784 --> 00:57:02,252
My dish is called

1435
00:57:02,752 --> 00:57:05,922
Kim Poong, So What? Cry Over My <i>Sujebi.</i>

1436
00:57:07,056 --> 00:57:09,759
-Kim Poong, So What?
-Kim Poong, So What?

1437
00:57:09,826 --> 00:57:11,528
Cry Over My <i>Sujebi.</i>

1438
00:57:11,594 --> 00:57:13,663
-Like how kids go, "So what?"
-Kim Poong, So What? Cry Over My <i>Sujebi.</i>

1439
00:57:13,730 --> 00:57:16,599
Kim Poong, So What? Cry Over My <i>Sujebi.</i>

1440
00:57:16,666 --> 00:57:18,802
That's how kids tease one another.

1441
00:57:18,868 --> 00:57:20,804
-That's from four years ago.
-So, <i>sujebi?</i>

1442
00:57:20,870 --> 00:57:23,907
I'm going to make my own <i>tteokbokki.</i>

1443
00:57:23,973 --> 00:57:27,677
<i>-Tteokbokki?</i>
-But I won't make it with gochujang.

1444
00:57:27,744 --> 00:57:29,946
Do you know oil <i>tteokbokki</i>,
famous at Tongin Market?

1445
00:57:30,013 --> 00:57:31,514
-That's a good one.
-Oil <i>tteokbokki?</i>

1446
00:57:31,581 --> 00:57:32,615
I'll make oil <i>sujebi,</i> pulled dough.

1447
00:57:32,682 --> 00:57:33,917
What?

1448
00:57:33,983 --> 00:57:35,218
SUJEBI?

1449
00:57:35,285 --> 00:57:37,086
I'll also make a side dish

1450
00:57:37,153 --> 00:57:38,288
of cheese balls.

1451
00:57:38,354 --> 00:57:41,291
-Cheese balls?
-I prepared those two dishes

1452
00:57:41,357 --> 00:57:42,759
to defeat this evil.

1453
00:57:42,826 --> 00:57:44,294
I WILL GET OVER MY POONGPHOBIA!

1454
00:57:45,228 --> 00:57:47,230
WEBTOON ARTIST POONG'S INGREDIENTS:
BEAN SPROUTS, CUCUMBER, CARROT

1455
00:57:47,297 --> 00:57:49,299
PORK NECK, SHRIMP, NOODLES, TOMATO SAUCE,
LOW-SUGAR CHOGOCHUJANG, PEANUT BUTTER

1456
00:57:49,365 --> 00:57:51,868
Poong, you're up against him.
What are you making?

1457
00:57:51,935 --> 00:57:53,937
The name of my dish is this.

1458
00:57:54,003 --> 00:57:56,706
Poongphobia Is Unstoppable! Triggered?

1459
00:57:56,773 --> 00:57:58,808
What Can You Do?

1460
00:57:58,875 --> 00:58:00,477
-Oh, my.
-That's it.

1461
00:58:00,543 --> 00:58:02,679
I've heard that from somewhere.

1462
00:58:02,745 --> 00:58:04,681
HE MUST'VE BEEN A TOUGH KID TO BRING UP

1463
00:58:04,747 --> 00:58:06,182
What are you making?

1464
00:58:06,249 --> 00:58:08,218
<i>-Napjak mandu, </i>flatplings.
-Flatplings.

1465
00:58:08,284 --> 00:58:10,620
-That sounds good.
-I'll make it

1466
00:58:10,687 --> 00:58:14,624
with a simplified recipe
for <i>cong you bing.</i>

1467
00:58:14,691 --> 00:58:16,593
What's that?

1468
00:58:16,659 --> 00:58:18,595
-Scallion pancakes.
-Scallion pancakes.

1469
00:58:18,661 --> 00:58:21,631
They come in many layers like pastry,

1470
00:58:21,698 --> 00:58:25,268
but I'll make one layer
and make <i>napjak mandu.</i>

1471
00:58:25,335 --> 00:58:28,137
Also, <i>bibim-somyeon,</i> seasoned noodles.

1472
00:58:28,204 --> 00:58:31,241
-Chef Sam had made it once before.
-I did.

1473
00:58:31,875 --> 00:58:34,811
IN EPISODE 68, SAM KIM MADE
KIM POONG'S CHOGOCHUJANG PASTA

1474
00:58:34,878 --> 00:58:36,880
<i>He had made use of my recipe.</i>

1475
00:58:36,946 --> 00:58:38,047
And I fought against him.

1476
00:58:38,114 --> 00:58:40,683
-Yes, he was up against Nam-no.
-You were against Nam-no?

1477
00:58:40,750 --> 00:58:42,986
Yes, and he won a star.

1478
00:58:43,052 --> 00:58:45,955
-I did.
-But I'll upgrade the recipe.

1479
00:58:46,022 --> 00:58:48,291
-That one was good.
-The <i>chogochujang</i> pasta?

1480
00:58:48,358 --> 00:58:51,227
Also, I've soaked the wheat noodles.

1481
00:58:51,294 --> 00:58:53,062
-Isn't that the fad?
-If you soak the noodles

1482
00:58:53,129 --> 00:58:56,266
before cooking them,
they'll cook in 30 seconds,

1483
00:58:56,332 --> 00:58:58,902
and they stay chewy much longer.

1484
00:58:58,968 --> 00:59:02,605
-I didn't know. Is that true?
-So I'll cook the noodles differently too.

1485
00:59:02,672 --> 00:59:04,507
-Look at him.
-He seems serious.

1486
00:59:04,574 --> 00:59:06,109
-No fun.
-He's like an actual chef.

1487
00:59:06,175 --> 00:59:10,146
That was the most boring presentation
of his dish in the history of the show.

1488
00:59:10,213 --> 00:59:11,614
I agree.

1489
00:59:11,681 --> 00:59:13,182
He doesn't want to joke around Nam-no.

1490
00:59:13,249 --> 00:59:15,785
Why do you only do this to me?

1491
00:59:15,852 --> 00:59:17,854
I'm not looking to lose today.

1492
00:59:17,921 --> 00:59:19,923
Let us begin the next round for Jung-an,

1493
00:59:19,989 --> 00:59:24,027
Eats Like a Kid, Chae Jung-an Core.

1494
00:59:24,093 --> 00:59:26,729
The headbutting master of the show

1495
00:59:26,796 --> 00:59:30,366
who's here to headbutt Kim Poong,
Chef Yoon Nam-no.

1496
00:59:31,167 --> 00:59:34,470
Here to destroy Yoon Nam-no again,
our show's…

1497
00:59:35,471 --> 00:59:37,974
<i>Wah, wah, wah, wah, wah</i>

1498
00:59:38,341 --> 00:59:39,542
THE ECHO GUY

1499
00:59:40,443 --> 00:59:42,712
…Webtoon Artist Kim Poong.

1500
00:59:42,779 --> 00:59:46,616
Will Nam-no
get over his Poongphobia today?

1501
00:59:46,683 --> 00:59:48,885
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1502
00:59:50,453 --> 00:59:51,754
NAM-NO'S ADVENTURE BEGINS!

1503
00:59:52,422 --> 00:59:55,558
How come
he has never won against him once?

1504
00:59:55,625 --> 00:59:56,626
Right?

1505
00:59:56,693 --> 00:59:58,761
Nam-no grinds bread as soon as he begins.

1506
00:59:58,828 --> 00:59:59,996
Flour, egg, breadcrumbs.

1507
01:00:00,063 --> 01:00:02,599
-The egg yolks are strikingly yellow.
-The yolks.

1508
01:00:03,166 --> 01:00:04,367
Green onion oil.

1509
01:00:04,434 --> 01:00:06,102
-Poong's chili oil.
-Poong's chili oil.

1510
01:00:06,169 --> 01:00:09,305
I said he was boring already,
and he's doing the same thing again.

1511
01:00:09,372 --> 01:00:10,340
-Chili oil?
-Chili oil.

1512
01:00:10,406 --> 01:00:11,841
You made Wellington again too!

1513
01:00:12,075 --> 01:00:13,676
AN IMMATURE FIGHT BREAKS OUT

1514
01:00:13,743 --> 01:00:14,611
So true.

1515
01:00:14,677 --> 01:00:16,112
All you make is Wellington.

1516
01:00:16,179 --> 01:00:17,880
I only did that twice.

1517
01:00:17,947 --> 01:00:19,649
You've made this at least 20 times.

1518
01:00:19,716 --> 01:00:21,050
Twenty times?

1519
01:00:21,117 --> 01:00:23,119
-More like 50 times.
-Probably.

1520
01:00:23,186 --> 01:00:24,387
What do you mean?

1521
01:00:24,454 --> 01:00:26,122
It tastes different each time.

1522
01:00:26,189 --> 01:00:27,390
Yes, they do taste different.

1523
01:00:27,557 --> 01:00:30,093
1. MAKE POONG'S CHILI OIL
FOR THE 23RD TIME

1524
01:00:30,159 --> 01:00:31,594
Can you alert me in five minutes?

1525
01:00:31,661 --> 01:00:34,063
-For the bean sprouts.
-Are you cooking bean sprouts?

1526
01:00:34,130 --> 01:00:37,033
Do children eat <i>napjak mandu?</i>

1527
01:00:37,100 --> 01:00:39,202
-It's what adults eat.
-What do you mean?

1528
01:00:39,268 --> 01:00:41,004
-Kids love it.
-It's not a dish for a kid.

1529
01:00:41,070 --> 01:00:43,573
-It's for an adult who eats like a kid.
-I'm sorry.

1530
01:00:43,640 --> 01:00:44,941
So it's okay.

1531
01:00:45,141 --> 01:00:47,243
NAM-NO GRABS THE MUGWORT TTEOK

1532
01:00:48,745 --> 01:00:51,247
Wait, that <i>tteok</i> is tough to get.

1533
01:00:51,981 --> 01:00:55,985
They're only made a few times a year,
in small batches.

1534
01:00:56,052 --> 01:00:58,054
-And they sent it to you?
-Yes.

1535
01:00:58,121 --> 01:00:59,889
-It's mugwort <i>tteok.</i>
-I wonder what it's like.

1536
01:00:59,956 --> 01:01:03,292
-That <i>tteok…</i>
-He's wrapping cheese with it.

1537
01:01:03,359 --> 01:01:04,260
With <i>tteok.</i>

1538
01:01:04,327 --> 01:01:05,828
-Is that brie?
-That's right.

1539
01:01:05,895 --> 01:01:07,263
Is he putting cheese in <i>tteok?</i>

1540
01:01:07,330 --> 01:01:11,067
That'll taste rich and delicious
when it's deep-fried.

1541
01:01:11,300 --> 01:01:12,702
INTRIGUED

1542
01:01:12,769 --> 01:01:14,303
Won't that pop in hot oil?

1543
01:01:14,370 --> 01:01:16,939
He'll put breadcrumbs on it.

1544
01:01:17,006 --> 01:01:18,408
-On <i>tteok?</i>
-That sounds good.

1545
01:01:18,474 --> 01:01:19,342
It looks good.

1546
01:01:19,409 --> 01:01:20,643
Salt.

1547
01:01:20,710 --> 01:01:22,378
That would've been bad. Salt.

1548
01:01:22,445 --> 01:01:24,647
-Flour. Are you making dough?
-I am.

1549
01:01:25,148 --> 01:01:27,450
It must be the scallion pancake batter.

1550
01:01:27,517 --> 01:01:28,751
It's way too thin.

1551
01:01:29,318 --> 01:01:30,253
It's too thin.

1552
01:01:30,319 --> 01:01:31,688
I knew it.

1553
01:01:31,888 --> 01:01:32,722
ADDING MORE FLOUR

1554
01:01:33,523 --> 01:01:37,794
Chef Hyun-suk seemed to know
it was too thin just by the sound.

1555
01:01:37,860 --> 01:01:40,029
I can tell by the way it spins.

1556
01:01:40,096 --> 01:01:41,164
AMAZED

1557
01:01:41,230 --> 01:01:44,600
That's the difference
between a longtime chef and a copycat.

1558
01:01:45,168 --> 01:01:46,536
-He heard it and knew?
-Yes.

1559
01:01:46,602 --> 01:01:48,971
I think he's making a scallion pancake.

1560
01:01:49,038 --> 01:01:50,306
It's still too wet.

1561
01:01:50,373 --> 01:01:52,275
-It's too wet.
-He coats it with more flour.

1562
01:01:52,341 --> 01:01:54,877
THROWS IT INTO THE FLOUR

1563
01:01:54,944 --> 01:01:56,846
-Look at him.
-What's he doing?

1564
01:01:56,913 --> 01:01:59,215
-Doesn't he look clumsy?
-Yes.

1565
01:01:59,282 --> 01:02:00,917
We have Poong's attacker here.

1566
01:02:00,983 --> 01:02:03,152
The hater's hateful comments.

1567
01:02:03,219 --> 01:02:04,787
-You're using the mandolin wrong.
-Right?

1568
01:02:04,854 --> 01:02:06,322
He's using it the other way.

1569
01:02:06,389 --> 01:02:08,658
He's supposed to push it out.

1570
01:02:08,725 --> 01:02:11,861
-He does seem a bit clumsy.
-Right?

1571
01:02:11,928 --> 01:02:13,429
That's how scary he is.

1572
01:02:13,496 --> 01:02:16,165
He lowers your expectations
by looking clumsy,

1573
01:02:16,232 --> 01:02:18,434
then captures you
with an edge in the flavor.

1574
01:02:18,501 --> 01:02:20,503
-That's his strategy.
-I see.

1575
01:02:20,570 --> 01:02:21,804
Don't let him fool you.

1576
01:02:21,871 --> 01:02:24,640
Yes, he has already won ten times.

1577
01:02:24,874 --> 01:02:26,409
WHAT'S NAM-NO UP TO?

1578
01:02:26,609 --> 01:02:28,711
HE MADE TWO CHEESE BALLS

1579
01:02:28,778 --> 01:02:29,846
How many are you making?

1580
01:02:29,912 --> 01:02:31,280
What? No idea.

1581
01:02:31,347 --> 01:02:32,982
How come you don't know?

1582
01:02:33,049 --> 01:02:35,818
I think he loses
because he makes such big portions.

1583
01:02:35,885 --> 01:02:37,954
She can't eat that much.

1584
01:02:38,020 --> 01:02:39,856
Don't make portions for yourself.

1585
01:02:39,922 --> 01:02:42,024
You don't have to make it for yourself.

1586
01:02:42,091 --> 01:02:43,426
-Okay.
-Get to the other stuff.

1587
01:02:43,493 --> 01:02:44,727
-Just make three.
-Okay.

1588
01:02:44,794 --> 01:02:46,629
He's making it for himself.

1589
01:02:46,696 --> 01:02:47,530
Just three.

1590
01:02:47,597 --> 01:02:49,599
EVERYONE LOVES NAM-NO

1591
01:02:49,665 --> 01:02:51,033
He's deep-frying them.

1592
01:02:51,100 --> 01:02:54,137
He puts cheese in the <i>tteok,</i>
coats it with breadcrumbs, and deep-fries.

1593
01:02:54,203 --> 01:02:55,271
Wow.

1594
01:02:55,338 --> 01:02:57,373
Poong's slicing lots of vegetables.

1595
01:02:57,440 --> 01:02:59,976
Yes, I'm trying to add some texture.

1596
01:03:00,042 --> 01:03:01,511
But kids don't eat veggies.

1597
01:03:01,577 --> 01:03:04,313
-Kids don't like veggies.
-They don't.

1598
01:03:04,380 --> 01:03:05,748
I may eat like a kid,

1599
01:03:05,815 --> 01:03:07,817
but I should eat veggies
for my blood sugar level.

1600
01:03:07,884 --> 01:03:09,252
So it's fine?

1601
01:03:09,318 --> 01:03:11,487
She's not actually
an elementary school kid.

1602
01:03:11,554 --> 01:03:15,024
You have ten minutes left.
Five minutes have passed.

1603
01:03:15,558 --> 01:03:16,726
GREEN ONION

1604
01:03:16,793 --> 01:03:17,827
Whoa.

1605
01:03:18,661 --> 01:03:20,062
Look at him chop.

1606
01:03:20,129 --> 01:03:21,564
Whoa.

1607
01:03:21,631 --> 01:03:22,965
Look.

1608
01:03:23,032 --> 01:03:25,301
Not a single piece is in the same width.

1609
01:03:25,368 --> 01:03:26,502
THROWING HATE COMMENTS!

1610
01:03:26,569 --> 01:03:29,672
-You're scary.
-He surely is fast.

1611
01:03:29,739 --> 01:03:31,140
-You might get hurt.
-But are they even?

1612
01:03:31,207 --> 01:03:32,508
He's just chopping it randomly.

1613
01:03:32,575 --> 01:03:33,743
-He's just battering.
-Yes.

1614
01:03:33,810 --> 01:03:35,244
-Battering it.
-Battering.

1615
01:03:35,311 --> 01:03:37,613
-He makes me feel anxious.
-The other way around.

1616
01:03:37,880 --> 01:03:39,382
HOW DOES AN ACTUAL CHEF CHOP?

1617
01:03:39,682 --> 01:03:41,384
GREEN ONION

1618
01:03:42,451 --> 01:03:43,753
GARLIC

1619
01:03:43,820 --> 01:03:45,888
He's finely chopping lots of garlic.

1620
01:03:45,955 --> 01:03:47,623
Do you need a lot?

1621
01:03:47,690 --> 01:03:50,193
-That's a lot of garlic.
-Garlic.

1622
01:03:51,127 --> 01:03:52,929
THIS TIME, POONG CHOPS MEAT

1623
01:03:55,898 --> 01:03:59,135
Are you cutting meat into uneven pieces
to create different textures?

1624
01:03:59,402 --> 01:04:00,570
THE HATE COMMENTS KEEP COMING

1625
01:04:00,636 --> 01:04:02,371
Is that why?

1626
01:04:02,438 --> 01:04:04,607
-Sure.
-It's practically getting ripped.

1627
01:04:05,474 --> 01:04:06,742
It's all for the texture.

1628
01:04:06,809 --> 01:04:08,878
-What now?
-It's getting ripped.

1629
01:04:08,945 --> 01:04:10,513
-He makes me feel anxious.
-Doesn't he?

1630
01:04:10,580 --> 01:04:13,549
Is he doing that
because it's hard to make even pieces?

1631
01:04:13,616 --> 01:04:15,351
It's the pork neck.

1632
01:04:15,418 --> 01:04:18,087
Sam used to cook as a naturalist, right?

1633
01:04:18,154 --> 01:04:19,856
Poong cooks as a liberalist.

1634
01:04:19,922 --> 01:04:21,657
-Liberalist.
-Deconstructionist.

1635
01:04:21,724 --> 01:04:22,692
He's free-spirited.

1636
01:04:23,059 --> 01:04:24,060
POONG WITH 5 WINS RIPS PORK NECK

1637
01:04:24,126 --> 01:04:25,261
It's so hard to cut.

1638
01:04:25,328 --> 01:04:28,564
When you look at the process,
Nam-no surely should be winning.

1639
01:04:28,631 --> 01:04:31,868
-But he has lost every single time.
-Exactly.

1640
01:04:31,934 --> 01:04:33,703
He must be heartbroken.

1641
01:04:34,003 --> 01:04:35,805
FOCUSED BREATHING!

1642
01:04:36,606 --> 01:04:37,840
POWER UP

1643
01:04:37,907 --> 01:04:39,075
SUGAR

1644
01:04:39,141 --> 01:04:41,544
Soy sauce, sugar, red pepper powder.

1645
01:04:41,611 --> 01:04:43,913
-And is that sesame oil?
-Yes.

1646
01:04:43,980 --> 01:04:46,382
It must be the sauce for oil <i>tteokbokki.</i>

1647
01:04:46,449 --> 01:04:48,150
4. MIX CONDIMENTS WITH GREEN ONION, GARLIC

1648
01:04:49,452 --> 01:04:51,420
Eight minutes left.

1649
01:04:51,487 --> 01:04:54,924
Poong hasn't done much so far.

1650
01:04:54,991 --> 01:04:57,393
-I'll just grind them.
-He's still prepping ingredients.

1651
01:04:57,460 --> 01:04:59,495
-He'll grind them.
-It doesn't work well

1652
01:04:59,562 --> 01:05:02,365
if you throw in
long pieces of green onion.

1653
01:05:02,431 --> 01:05:03,799
ONLY HAS EYES ON POONG

1654
01:05:03,866 --> 01:05:06,235
Hyun-suk only watches Poong.

1655
01:05:06,302 --> 01:05:07,904
He's trying to destroy him.

1656
01:05:08,204 --> 01:05:10,573
5. GRIND SHRIMP AND GREEN ONION

1657
01:05:10,640 --> 01:05:12,742
The deep-fried <i>tteok</i> is done.

1658
01:05:12,808 --> 01:05:14,710
-Did it turn out well?
-It didn't pop.

1659
01:05:14,777 --> 01:05:17,446
-They look good.
-They're fried very nicely.

1660
01:05:17,513 --> 01:05:20,049
-Cheese?
-He's grating cheese.

1661
01:05:20,116 --> 01:05:23,052
-That's right.
-He's making cheese tuile.

1662
01:05:23,119 --> 01:05:24,520
That's grana padano.

1663
01:05:25,254 --> 01:05:26,689
Seven minutes.

1664
01:05:26,756 --> 01:05:28,257
-Grab the bean sprouts.
-Bean sprouts.

1665
01:05:28,591 --> 01:05:30,626
BEAN SPROUTS COOKED FOR 5 MINUTES

1666
01:05:30,893 --> 01:05:33,329
6. COOL COOKED BEAN SPROUTS IN ICE WATER

1667
01:05:34,964 --> 01:05:35,865
FINALLY, THE DUMPLING SKIN!

1668
01:05:35,932 --> 01:05:37,700
That's the scallion pancake dough.

1669
01:05:37,767 --> 01:05:38,801
Scallion pancake dough.

1670
01:05:38,868 --> 01:05:40,736
-I see.
-Will he deep-fry that?

1671
01:05:40,803 --> 01:05:42,939
-He's supposed to toast it.
-Did it turn out nicely?

1672
01:05:43,005 --> 01:05:46,642
-The dough looks great.
-Does it? Excellent.

1673
01:05:46,709 --> 01:05:48,144
The dough looks great,

1674
01:05:48,210 --> 01:05:50,479
but he's not flattening it evenly.

1675
01:05:50,546 --> 01:05:53,416
They both seem serious today.
No one's talking.

1676
01:05:53,482 --> 01:05:56,285
Hyun-suk seems to be
in charge of comic relief.

1677
01:05:56,352 --> 01:05:57,853
No, I'm in charge of cooking.

1678
01:05:58,454 --> 01:05:59,956
The comic relief is another guy.

1679
01:06:00,022 --> 01:06:01,958
Jong-won has been making an effort lately.

1680
01:06:02,024 --> 01:06:04,026
-That's right.
-The comic relief.

1681
01:06:04,093 --> 01:06:05,227
Six minutes.

1682
01:06:05,294 --> 01:06:06,896
-Six minutes?
-Six minutes left.

1683
01:06:06,963 --> 01:06:09,098
-Emergency.
-Only six minutes.

1684
01:06:09,165 --> 01:06:09,999
Green onion.

1685
01:06:10,066 --> 01:06:12,101
-Sprinkle green onion.
-Sprinkle green onion.

1686
01:06:12,168 --> 01:06:14,003
-Shrimp.
-Shrimp.

1687
01:06:14,070 --> 01:06:16,405
-Shrimp.
-Shrimp?

1688
01:06:17,606 --> 01:06:19,842
Only five minutes remain.

1689
01:06:20,576 --> 01:06:23,145
Will the <i>sujebi</i> get cooked in time?

1690
01:06:23,212 --> 01:06:24,280
It cooks very quickly.

1691
01:06:24,347 --> 01:06:25,815
Dough made with water cooks quickly.

1692
01:06:25,881 --> 01:06:27,717
He's just boiling them briefly.

1693
01:06:27,783 --> 01:06:29,485
I guess he'll boil it first.

1694
01:06:29,552 --> 01:06:32,788
-Fold.
-That's how you make a scallion pancake?

1695
01:06:32,855 --> 01:06:35,858
It's not. I'm working off of the recipe.

1696
01:06:35,925 --> 01:06:38,794
-He's rolling it out again.
-That looks like a towel.

1697
01:06:38,861 --> 01:06:40,296
8. FOLD IN HALF, ROLL OUT, AND CHOP

1698
01:06:41,764 --> 01:06:43,165
-There's the <i>sujebi.</i>
<i>-Sujebi</i> sounds good.

1699
01:06:43,232 --> 01:06:44,166
Five minutes.

1700
01:06:44,233 --> 01:06:46,002
-Yes.
-Jung-hwan.

1701
01:06:46,068 --> 01:06:49,038
Will he overcome his Poongphobia?

1702
01:06:49,438 --> 01:06:51,640
RIBEYE

1703
01:06:52,041 --> 01:06:53,075
Meat?

1704
01:06:53,976 --> 01:06:56,612
-For <i>tteokbokki?</i>
-Nam-no grinds meat.

1705
01:06:57,380 --> 01:06:59,215
It's going into the sauce.

1706
01:06:59,281 --> 01:07:01,150
<i>Sujebi</i> pieces are done.

1707
01:07:01,217 --> 01:07:04,286
-He's cooking beef in the sauce.
-That looks good.

1708
01:07:04,353 --> 01:07:07,023
I guess he'll add the <i>sujebi</i> here.

1709
01:07:07,089 --> 01:07:08,624
-You're right.
-I see.

1710
01:07:08,691 --> 01:07:11,060
-Are your <i>napjak mandu </i>done?
-Yes.

1711
01:07:11,127 --> 01:07:15,998
I hope these flatplings become a success.
He has 4 minutes and 20 seconds.

1712
01:07:16,065 --> 01:07:17,299
You have to make the sauce too.

1713
01:07:17,366 --> 01:07:18,434
Four minutes.

1714
01:07:18,501 --> 01:07:21,404
-The sauce.
-First, wheat noodles.

1715
01:07:22,004 --> 01:07:23,372
-Careful.
-It's dripping.

1716
01:07:23,572 --> 01:07:24,840
POURING

1717
01:07:25,741 --> 01:07:27,410
-What's that?
-What is that?

1718
01:07:27,476 --> 01:07:29,278
Soaked wheat noodles.

1719
01:07:29,345 --> 01:07:30,980
-Four minutes.
-Stir the noodles!

1720
01:07:31,047 --> 01:07:32,748
-Four minutes.
-Keep stirring.

1721
01:07:32,815 --> 01:07:34,850
-Leave it there and stir-fry.
-Hurry up and stir-fry.

1722
01:07:34,917 --> 01:07:38,254
He has to add the pork neck soon.

1723
01:07:38,320 --> 01:07:40,256
-Hurry up.
-Four minutes.

1724
01:07:40,322 --> 01:07:42,024
-Stir-fry that quickly.
-Four minutes.

1725
01:07:42,091 --> 01:07:44,226
-Hurry.
-Jung-an can't eat that much, Nam-no.

1726
01:07:44,693 --> 01:07:45,594
THEN I WILL

1727
01:07:45,661 --> 01:07:46,662
It looks like ragu pasta.

1728
01:07:47,396 --> 01:07:48,464
Jung-hwan.

1729
01:07:48,531 --> 01:07:51,834
-Can you taste that?
-The <i>tteokbokki</i> sauce.

1730
01:07:51,901 --> 01:07:54,236
-The oil <i>sujebi.</i>
-There's no time.

1731
01:07:54,303 --> 01:07:57,239
Can Nam-no headbutt Poong
with that flavor?

1732
01:07:57,306 --> 01:07:58,674
He surely can.

1733
01:07:59,975 --> 01:08:02,478
-He'll headbutt him.
-It looks delicious.

1734
01:08:02,545 --> 01:08:05,181
It's the kind of flavor
that kids would love.

1735
01:08:05,247 --> 01:08:06,415
Isn't it salty?

1736
01:08:06,482 --> 01:08:10,653
It's a bit salty, but not spicy,
and it has a little bit of sweetness.

1737
01:08:10,719 --> 01:08:11,754
I think she'll like it.

1738
01:08:11,821 --> 01:08:13,856
Dang it. The pork neck.

1739
01:08:13,923 --> 01:08:14,757
What will you do?

1740
01:08:15,024 --> 01:08:16,459
LESS THAN 4 MINUTES LEFT

1741
01:08:16,525 --> 01:08:18,260
Wait, I have to save these first.

1742
01:08:18,327 --> 01:08:22,064
He'll save the flatplings first.
He's flipping them.

1743
01:08:22,131 --> 01:08:23,899
Oh, whatever. I'll put them in.

1744
01:08:24,300 --> 01:08:25,267
WHAT'S HE DOING?

1745
01:08:25,334 --> 01:08:26,836
He just threw the meat in there.

1746
01:08:26,902 --> 01:08:28,504
The heat doesn't look high enough.

1747
01:08:29,138 --> 01:08:30,506
JUNG-AN FEELS NERVOUS

1748
01:08:30,573 --> 01:08:32,708
Wait, the strainer.

1749
01:08:32,775 --> 01:08:35,878
-The strainer.
-That's not the Poong I know.

1750
01:08:37,413 --> 01:08:38,414
The sauce.

1751
01:08:38,481 --> 01:08:39,648
The sauce?

1752
01:08:39,715 --> 01:08:41,517
The sauce? Where's the sauce?

1753
01:08:41,584 --> 01:08:43,352
Right here. The onion.

1754
01:08:43,419 --> 01:08:45,020
That bowl is way too small.

1755
01:08:45,087 --> 01:08:46,555
You really don't have time.

1756
01:08:46,622 --> 01:08:48,090
Peanut butter.

1757
01:08:48,157 --> 01:08:49,992
11. MIX TOMATO SAUCE, CHOGOCHUJANG,
AND MORE

1758
01:08:50,059 --> 01:08:51,560
What's Poong making right now?

1759
01:08:51,627 --> 01:08:53,329
-The sauce.
-The sauce?

1760
01:08:53,395 --> 01:08:55,264
-The noodle sauce, Jung-hwan.
-The noodle sauce.

1761
01:08:55,331 --> 01:08:58,667
-It looks good.
-The sauce is splashing out of the bowl.

1762
01:08:58,734 --> 01:09:01,670
-He's spilling everything. How clumsy.
-That's a lot.

1763
01:09:01,737 --> 01:09:03,372
He's usually not that bad, right?

1764
01:09:03,439 --> 01:09:04,640
No.

1765
01:09:04,707 --> 01:09:06,208
He's doing that?

1766
01:09:06,275 --> 01:09:07,376
Jung-hwan, the sauce.

1767
01:09:07,443 --> 01:09:09,979
I can't bother him,
so let me try the sauce.

1768
01:09:10,045 --> 01:09:12,047
-What's this sauce?
-They're cooked.

1769
01:09:12,114 --> 01:09:13,549
-It looks good.
-It does.

1770
01:09:13,616 --> 01:09:14,950
-It looks good.
-Scallion pancakes.

1771
01:09:15,017 --> 01:09:19,455
-It's the noodle sauce.
-Do you think Poong will win again?

1772
01:09:19,522 --> 01:09:21,090
Will Poong win again?

1773
01:09:21,157 --> 01:09:22,558
I think so.

1774
01:09:22,625 --> 01:09:23,959
I think so.

1775
01:09:25,060 --> 01:09:26,729
This sauce is amazing.

1776
01:09:26,795 --> 01:09:29,565
Are you serious?
He just poured random stuff in.

1777
01:09:29,632 --> 01:09:31,767
It turned out well. But how?

1778
01:09:31,834 --> 01:09:33,402
It tastes like basil.

1779
01:09:33,469 --> 01:09:34,670
Does it?

1780
01:09:34,737 --> 01:09:37,973
-The tomato sauce.
-The tomato sauce had a hint of basil.

1781
01:09:38,040 --> 01:09:39,408
I KNEW IT

1782
01:09:39,475 --> 01:09:42,211
-The sauce does half of the lifting.
-Your hair.

1783
01:09:42,278 --> 01:09:44,480
12. CHILL NOODLES AND WRING THEM OUT

1784
01:09:45,014 --> 01:09:46,815
NAM-NO MAKES THE SAUCE TOO

1785
01:09:46,882 --> 01:09:47,950
What are you making?

1786
01:09:48,017 --> 01:09:49,585
-What is it?
-Cheese sauce.

1787
01:09:49,652 --> 01:09:50,920
-You have to blend that.
-Yes.

1788
01:09:51,754 --> 01:09:53,155
A minute left.

1789
01:09:53,222 --> 01:09:54,657
It's all right.

1790
01:09:54,723 --> 01:09:56,325
Add the noodles.

1791
01:09:56,392 --> 01:09:58,794
That noodle dish looks delicious.

1792
01:09:58,861 --> 01:10:00,429
The pork neck continues to cook.

1793
01:10:00,496 --> 01:10:03,999
-You have one minute left.
-One minute.

1794
01:10:04,066 --> 01:10:05,367
-Wrap it up.
-Start plating.

1795
01:10:06,368 --> 01:10:07,803
You need to wrap it up.

1796
01:10:07,870 --> 01:10:10,539
It looks hot. Is the pork neck burning?

1797
01:10:10,606 --> 01:10:12,208
-It's fine.
-It is?

1798
01:10:12,274 --> 01:10:14,710
HOW DID THIS TURN OUT SO WELL?

1799
01:10:14,777 --> 01:10:17,279
MUGWORT TTEOK CHEESE BALLS

1800
01:10:17,346 --> 01:10:20,883
10. PLATE CHEESE BALLS ON CHEESE SAUCE
AND TOP WITH LEMON ZEST

1801
01:10:20,950 --> 01:10:23,085
-Bean sprouts.
-Bean sprouts.

1802
01:10:23,152 --> 01:10:24,353
-What?
-Not those.

1803
01:10:24,420 --> 01:10:26,121
-What?
-Not those.

1804
01:10:26,188 --> 01:10:29,892
-Those are uncooked.
-Dang it!

1805
01:10:29,959 --> 01:10:31,760
Do you like raw bean sprouts?

1806
01:10:31,827 --> 01:10:33,529
-Raw?
-Raw.

1807
01:10:33,596 --> 01:10:34,563
Raw? Sure.

1808
01:10:34,630 --> 01:10:36,498
-Did he add raw bean sprouts?
-No, right?

1809
01:10:36,565 --> 01:10:39,101
-You cooked some earlier.
-Come on.

1810
01:10:39,168 --> 01:10:41,370
Is this your way of trying to be funny?

1811
01:10:42,404 --> 01:10:43,739
Grab the pork neck.

1812
01:10:43,806 --> 01:10:46,609
-Twenty seconds.
-Pork neck.

1813
01:10:46,675 --> 01:10:48,611
-Ring the bell first.
-Twenty seconds.

1814
01:10:48,677 --> 01:10:50,379
You're making noodles,
so ring the bell first.

1815
01:10:50,446 --> 01:10:52,448
The faster you finish,
the more advantageous it is.

1816
01:10:52,915 --> 01:10:55,217
I ONLY SERVE A QUARTER OF WHAT I MAKE

1817
01:10:55,284 --> 01:10:56,619
Ten seconds.

1818
01:10:56,685 --> 01:10:57,886
-The strainer.
-Nine.

1819
01:10:58,454 --> 01:10:59,622
-Eight. Seven.
-Eight. Seven.

1820
01:10:59,922 --> 01:11:01,824
FRIED PORK NECK

1821
01:11:02,191 --> 01:11:05,361
14. TOP NOODLES WITH FRIED PORK NECK

1822
01:11:05,427 --> 01:11:06,562
Nine.

1823
01:11:06,629 --> 01:11:08,564
-Eight. Seven.
-Eight. Seven.

1824
01:11:08,797 --> 01:11:10,499
LASTLY, THE CHEESE TUILE

1825
01:11:11,367 --> 01:11:13,502
TOPPED ON THE OIL SUJEBI

1826
01:11:13,569 --> 01:11:15,938
One! Nam-no finishes first.

1827
01:11:16,005 --> 01:11:18,440
-Nam-no's done.
-Where's mine?

1828
01:11:18,507 --> 01:11:21,210
-The flatplings are here.
-Where are they? Right here.

1829
01:11:21,910 --> 01:11:24,580
ONLY ALLOWED TO
PLATE THE THINGS THAT ARE ALREADY DONE

1830
01:11:24,647 --> 01:11:27,383
-Poong is done too.
-He's done too.

1831
01:11:28,817 --> 01:11:30,386
-Wow.
-They're both done cooking.

1832
01:11:30,753 --> 01:11:33,088
COME TO THE CAFETERIA

1833
01:11:33,155 --> 01:11:35,524
He almost served that
with raw bean sprouts.

1834
01:11:36,325 --> 01:11:38,560
Didn't a few get inside?

1835
01:11:38,627 --> 01:11:41,764
Everything has been finished for Jung-an.

1836
01:11:41,830 --> 01:11:43,032
Do they look good?

1837
01:11:43,098 --> 01:11:45,501
I don't have a kid,
but I even want one right now.

1838
01:11:47,703 --> 01:11:48,804
HER UNPREDICTABLE SPEECH

1839
01:11:48,871 --> 01:11:50,072
So this is what she does.

1840
01:11:50,139 --> 01:11:52,508
I'm sorry,
but we can't help you with that.

1841
01:11:52,574 --> 01:11:54,109
Of course not.

1842
01:11:54,176 --> 01:11:55,944
-Try the dishes.
-All right.

1843
01:11:56,011 --> 01:11:58,180
Nam-no finished first,

1844
01:11:58,247 --> 01:12:00,249
so she'll try his dishes first.

1845
01:12:00,749 --> 01:12:03,819
CHEESE BALLS WRAPPED WITH MUGWORT TTEOK

1846
01:12:04,553 --> 01:12:07,656
AND OIL SUJEBI
UNDERNEATH CRISPY CHEESE TUILE

1847
01:12:09,625 --> 01:12:13,228
CHEF YOON NAM-NO'S
KIM POONG, SO WHAT? CRY OVER MY SUJEBI

1848
01:12:13,295 --> 01:12:15,964
Break the cheese on top

1849
01:12:16,031 --> 01:12:18,467
and eat it with the <i>tteokbokki</i> underneath.

1850
01:12:18,534 --> 01:12:21,203
And then, try the cheese balls.

1851
01:12:21,270 --> 01:12:24,640
Nam-no, I think it'd be better
if she had the cheese balls first.

1852
01:12:24,707 --> 01:12:26,375
Then try the cheese balls first.

1853
01:12:26,442 --> 01:12:27,876
He changed the orders.

1854
01:12:27,943 --> 01:12:29,812
-Cheese balls first?
-Yes.

1855
01:12:29,878 --> 01:12:32,348
-Inside the <i>tteok…</i>
-He's definitely easily swayed.

1856
01:12:32,414 --> 01:12:34,750
-He'll lose because of that.
-There's cheese inside.

1857
01:12:34,817 --> 01:12:37,619
-It looks good.
-Kind of like an appetizer.

1858
01:12:37,686 --> 01:12:39,121
-It looks good.
-That looks good.

1859
01:12:39,188 --> 01:12:41,323
-That's mugwort <i>tteok.</i>
-Yes.

1860
01:12:41,390 --> 01:12:43,392
It's mugwort<i> tteok.</i>

1861
01:12:43,459 --> 01:12:44,660
The cheese melted nicely.

1862
01:12:45,194 --> 01:12:46,662
LOTS OF CHEESE SAUCE

1863
01:12:46,729 --> 01:12:47,963
It looks really good.

1864
01:12:48,030 --> 01:12:49,098
It has to be good.

1865
01:12:49,598 --> 01:12:52,234
Will it bring back her childlike appetite?

1866
01:12:52,301 --> 01:12:54,036
She likes mugwort <i>tteok,</i>

1867
01:12:54,103 --> 01:12:55,404
and it's filled with cheese.

1868
01:12:55,471 --> 01:12:57,573
Do you want to headbutt him?

1869
01:12:57,639 --> 01:12:58,574
She'll headbutt him.

1870
01:12:58,640 --> 01:13:00,075
Should I open up my forehead?

1871
01:13:01,143 --> 01:13:02,077
Get over here.

1872
01:13:03,379 --> 01:13:04,279
Get over here.

1873
01:13:04,346 --> 01:13:05,948
TEEHEE

1874
01:13:06,515 --> 01:13:08,684
YOU'VE DONE IT

1875
01:13:08,751 --> 01:13:10,052
It must be good.

1876
01:13:10,119 --> 01:13:11,487
It's a lovely taste.

1877
01:13:12,454 --> 01:13:13,989
-Just like Nam-no?
-Lovely?

1878
01:13:14,256 --> 01:13:16,859
THE LOVELY PIGGY

1879
01:13:16,925 --> 01:13:18,761
I don't want to headbutt anyone now.

1880
01:13:18,827 --> 01:13:21,463
-It makes me want to kiss.
-It makes her want to kiss.

1881
01:13:21,530 --> 01:13:22,998
It tastes lovely.

1882
01:13:23,065 --> 01:13:25,968
-And next.
-Oil <i>sujebi.</i>

1883
01:13:26,034 --> 01:13:27,035
-Oil <i>sujebi.</i>
-Oil <i>sujebi.</i>

1884
01:13:27,102 --> 01:13:30,806
Jung-hwan tried it and said
it was absolutely delicious.

1885
01:13:30,873 --> 01:13:32,941
-So break this and eat it together?
-Yes.

1886
01:13:33,008 --> 01:13:34,243
BREAK THE TUILE AND MIX IT

1887
01:13:34,309 --> 01:13:36,412
BREAKING

1888
01:13:36,478 --> 01:13:37,446
It's thick.

1889
01:13:37,513 --> 01:13:39,681
-This and <i>sujebi.</i>
-It looks good.

1890
01:13:39,748 --> 01:13:41,617
That's it. Try a bite.

1891
01:13:41,683 --> 01:13:43,419
YUM

1892
01:13:44,353 --> 01:13:45,721
CRUNCH

1893
01:13:46,622 --> 01:13:48,390
Will it bring back her childhood appetite?

1894
01:13:49,558 --> 01:13:50,526
CHUCKLE…

1895
01:13:51,193 --> 01:13:53,228
-Why are you chuckling?
-What is it?

1896
01:13:53,295 --> 01:13:54,296
Does it taste funny?

1897
01:13:54,863 --> 01:13:58,767
I wouldn't say this is something
that elementary school kids would like.

1898
01:13:58,834 --> 01:13:59,701
What?

1899
01:13:59,768 --> 01:14:01,770
Well, the kid must've gone through a lot.

1900
01:14:03,105 --> 01:14:04,373
What does that taste like?

1901
01:14:04,440 --> 01:14:07,142
I've gone through so much.

1902
01:14:07,209 --> 01:14:08,677
It's a kid that matured too early.

1903
01:14:08,944 --> 01:14:10,279
THE SENSITIVE GUY CAN CONNECT

1904
01:14:10,345 --> 01:14:11,947
Did the kid become street-smart too early?

1905
01:14:12,014 --> 01:14:14,383
-Too worldly.
-Too worldly.

1906
01:14:14,450 --> 01:14:15,684
Too worldly?

1907
01:14:15,751 --> 01:14:18,620
The first one tasted lovely,

1908
01:14:18,687 --> 01:14:23,425
but this one learned
the ups and downs of life too early.

1909
01:14:23,492 --> 01:14:25,294
An upperclassman. A sixth-grader.

1910
01:14:25,360 --> 01:14:29,264
The moment I had this cheese tuile,

1911
01:14:30,499 --> 01:14:31,834
it made me crave a drink.

1912
01:14:31,900 --> 01:14:33,769
-You wanted a drink?
-Yes.

1913
01:14:33,836 --> 01:14:35,604
-Just the cheese?
-Yes, like a bar snack.

1914
01:14:35,671 --> 01:14:38,774
I think she just likes to drink.

1915
01:14:38,841 --> 01:14:41,844
-It's past my usual drinking time.
-It's that hour.

1916
01:14:41,910 --> 01:14:44,480
-Oil <i>tteokbokki</i> is good with soju too.
-It's a bar snack.

1917
01:14:44,546 --> 01:14:45,814
Right?

1918
01:14:45,881 --> 01:14:47,649
It makes you crave a drink.

1919
01:14:47,716 --> 01:14:49,751
It's a bit too mature
to call it a kid's appetite.

1920
01:14:49,818 --> 01:14:51,720
-I see.
-Something that makes her crave soju.

1921
01:14:51,787 --> 01:14:56,558
Is there a difference
between <i>tteok </i>and <i>sujebi?</i>

1922
01:14:56,625 --> 01:14:59,661
The texture…
It was probably made of potato.

1923
01:14:59,728 --> 01:15:01,129
-It was potato <i>sujebi.</i>
-Right?

1924
01:15:01,196 --> 01:15:03,432
If you eat this knowing that,

1925
01:15:03,499 --> 01:15:06,602
it has a bite to it,
which reminds you of potatoes. It's fun.

1926
01:15:06,668 --> 01:15:08,971
It's a delicacy.

1927
01:15:09,037 --> 01:15:10,305
The next dish,

1928
01:15:10,372 --> 01:15:11,807
made by Webtoon Artist Kim Poong.

1929
01:15:12,574 --> 01:15:15,711
CHOGOCHUJANG TOMATO NOODLES

1930
01:15:16,712 --> 01:15:20,148
AND NAPJAK MANDU, FLATPLINGS,
MADE IN CONG YOU BING STYLE

1931
01:15:21,416 --> 01:15:25,120
KIM POONG'S POONGPHOBIA IS UNSTOPPABLE!
TRIGGERED? WHAT CAN YOU DO?

1932
01:15:25,187 --> 01:15:28,557
It's made
with many of my favorite ingredients.

1933
01:15:28,624 --> 01:15:31,126
-I love bean sprouts.
-Bean sprouts.

1934
01:15:31,193 --> 01:15:32,928
You almost used raw ones.

1935
01:15:32,995 --> 01:15:35,330
I would've eaten that too.

1936
01:15:35,931 --> 01:15:37,232
I'll try the noodles first.

1937
01:15:37,633 --> 01:15:39,835
A BITE OF THE NOODLES FIRST

1938
01:15:40,335 --> 01:15:41,670
There's no way it's good.

1939
01:15:41,737 --> 01:15:42,971
But it is.

1940
01:15:43,038 --> 01:15:45,474
-She seemed to want something strong.
-It's probably good.

1941
01:15:46,074 --> 01:15:47,309
Mmm.

1942
01:15:47,376 --> 01:15:49,645
This is something a kid would love.

1943
01:15:50,212 --> 01:15:51,980
-Did I lose again?
-And she wanted that.

1944
01:15:52,047 --> 01:15:54,750
I feel like a little kid again.

1945
01:15:54,816 --> 01:15:56,618
I want to go to an arcade.

1946
01:15:56,685 --> 01:15:57,953
It's good.

1947
01:15:58,020 --> 01:15:59,555
-It's good.
-You've done well, Nam-no.

1948
01:15:59,855 --> 01:16:01,223
NO WAY…

1949
01:16:01,290 --> 01:16:02,824
I want to try it too.

1950
01:16:02,891 --> 01:16:04,293
Me too.

1951
01:16:04,359 --> 01:16:07,396
-Put that on the <i>napjak mandu</i>.
-On the dumpling.

1952
01:16:07,462 --> 01:16:09,064
-Can she do that?
-Yes.

1953
01:16:09,131 --> 01:16:10,966
-Put it on top.
-That's the point.

1954
01:16:11,033 --> 01:16:12,935
What did you make?

1955
01:16:13,001 --> 01:16:14,970
<i>Napjak mandu</i>, flatplings. I fried them.

1956
01:16:15,037 --> 01:16:16,305
I see.

1957
01:16:17,472 --> 01:16:18,907
May I have a piece of meat?

1958
01:16:19,341 --> 01:16:20,175
PORK NECK

1959
01:16:21,410 --> 01:16:23,845
She hasn't had something like this before.

1960
01:16:23,912 --> 01:16:27,149
-She eats the dumpling and the noodles.
-All at once.

1961
01:16:28,350 --> 01:16:29,718
GRABBING WITH HER FINGERS

1962
01:16:30,285 --> 01:16:31,954
I'll just use my hands.

1963
01:16:32,020 --> 01:16:33,755
It contained water, so he had to cook it.

1964
01:16:34,456 --> 01:16:38,160
Will it bring back
your childhood appetite?

1965
01:16:39,695 --> 01:16:41,396
Mmm. It's good.

1966
01:16:41,463 --> 01:16:43,332
-It's good?
-It is.

1967
01:16:43,398 --> 01:16:46,635
How did the meat turn out like this?
It's really good.

1968
01:16:46,702 --> 01:16:48,737
-The meat is really good?
-The pork neck.

1969
01:16:48,804 --> 01:16:51,039
The emergency light goes off.

1970
01:16:51,106 --> 01:16:53,275
Do the noodles feel different than usual?

1971
01:16:53,342 --> 01:16:55,677
They're even milder. The noodles.

1972
01:16:55,744 --> 01:16:57,846
-It's a popular new method.
-It is?

1973
01:16:57,913 --> 01:17:01,350
It trended in Japan
about two or three years ago.

1974
01:17:01,416 --> 01:17:04,019
You can do the same with pasta too.

1975
01:17:04,086 --> 01:17:06,555
-Soaking pasta?
-Pasta?

1976
01:17:06,622 --> 01:17:08,023
What's the dumpling like?

1977
01:17:08,090 --> 01:17:11,226
If he didn't call it <i>mandu,</i>

1978
01:17:11,293 --> 01:17:13,729
I wouldn't have thought of it as one.

1979
01:17:13,795 --> 01:17:16,198
-It reminds me of naan.
-Naan.

1980
01:17:16,264 --> 01:17:20,669
Flatplings don't have fillings,

1981
01:17:20,736 --> 01:17:23,905
so it's basically just the skin,
and you wrap other food with it.

1982
01:17:23,972 --> 01:17:25,340
Between the two dishes,

1983
01:17:25,407 --> 01:17:28,276
which one seems like
it was made by a better person?

1984
01:17:28,343 --> 01:17:30,312
They're both not on the good side.

1985
01:17:30,679 --> 01:17:32,614
FIRST OFF, THEY BOTH AREN'T GOOD

1986
01:17:33,181 --> 01:17:34,750
They aren't good.

1987
01:17:34,816 --> 01:17:37,519
But he seems a bit relaxed.

1988
01:17:37,586 --> 01:17:39,121
Does this guy not seem relaxed?

1989
01:17:39,187 --> 01:17:40,222
-This guy?
-Yes.

1990
01:17:40,288 --> 01:17:41,723
This one is a rising star.

1991
01:17:41,790 --> 01:17:44,092
-A rising star?
-How can I put this?

1992
01:17:44,159 --> 01:17:46,795
He has no time to relax
because he's charging forward.

1993
01:17:46,862 --> 01:17:49,364
-That's what it feels like.
-Like a racehorse.

1994
01:17:49,431 --> 01:17:52,534
What does it taste like
for her to say those things?

1995
01:17:52,601 --> 01:17:55,003
Should we share some with Sang-uk too?

1996
01:17:55,070 --> 01:17:57,005
The sauce was delicious.

1997
01:17:57,072 --> 01:17:58,140
You can give it back to her.

1998
01:17:58,206 --> 01:18:00,075
Sang-uk began to eat it already.

1999
01:18:00,142 --> 01:18:02,110
He must've been very curious.

2000
01:18:02,177 --> 01:18:03,011
Mmm.

2001
01:18:03,078 --> 01:18:05,914
I think the texture adds to the flavor.

2002
01:18:06,548 --> 01:18:08,350
Mmm. It's good.

2003
01:18:08,417 --> 01:18:09,651
I get what you mean.

2004
01:18:09,718 --> 01:18:12,654
The texture
is completely different from <i>tteok.</i>

2005
01:18:12,721 --> 01:18:14,589
-It feels odd.
-A bit.

2006
01:18:14,656 --> 01:18:17,392
And this sauce is delicious.

2007
01:18:17,459 --> 01:18:19,494
-It's like bulgogi.
-Or <i>jeyuk-bokkeum.</i>

2008
01:18:19,561 --> 01:18:21,797
It's like adding <i>tteok</i> to bulgogi.

2009
01:18:21,863 --> 01:18:23,231
What do you think, Jong-won?

2010
01:18:23,298 --> 01:18:25,500
I love the oil <i>tteokbokki</i>
at Tongin Market.

2011
01:18:25,567 --> 01:18:29,371
It feels like it was made
in an alley outside of the market

2012
01:18:29,438 --> 01:18:30,605
by a rowdy granny.

2013
01:18:32,441 --> 01:18:33,475
"Stop eating!"

2014
01:18:34,576 --> 01:18:36,978
I think this is growing on me.

2015
01:18:37,045 --> 01:18:39,514
LOVES IT

2016
01:18:39,581 --> 01:18:43,885
I get why you called this lovely.

2017
01:18:43,952 --> 01:18:46,988
The texture felt great
as it entered my mouth.

2018
01:18:47,055 --> 01:18:49,958
I could feel your loveliness in the dish.

2019
01:18:50,025 --> 01:18:51,593
I see. Excellent.

2020
01:18:51,660 --> 01:18:54,296
Next, Poong's dish.

2021
01:18:56,031 --> 01:18:58,467
SLURP

2022
01:18:58,800 --> 01:19:00,702
YUM

2023
01:19:00,769 --> 01:19:03,972
It looks like it was made with gochujang,
but it's mostly tomato sauce.

2024
01:19:04,039 --> 01:19:05,340
Don't you smell basil?

2025
01:19:05,407 --> 01:19:07,109
This <i>jjolmyeon</i> is good.

2026
01:19:08,276 --> 01:19:10,011
I thought it'd be like <i>jjolmyeon,</i>

2027
01:19:10,078 --> 01:19:12,147
but instead of that, it felt like

2028
01:19:12,214 --> 01:19:13,815
a spicy Western dish.

2029
01:19:13,882 --> 01:19:15,650
It's Napolitan noodles.

2030
01:19:16,518 --> 01:19:21,056
It's closer to tomato spaghetti
than <i>bibim-guksu</i>.

2031
01:19:21,123 --> 01:19:22,791
It's like <i>bibim-guksu</i> Sam would've made.

2032
01:19:22,858 --> 01:19:24,359
Why me?

2033
01:19:24,426 --> 01:19:25,627
It doesn't taste too strong.

2034
01:19:25,694 --> 01:19:27,929
Even my daughter could have this.

2035
01:19:27,996 --> 01:19:29,598
It doesn't taste too strong.

2036
01:19:29,664 --> 01:19:33,401
I wanted to make it mild
if it were for an elementary school kid.

2037
01:19:33,468 --> 01:19:36,171
It won't be easy to choose.

2038
01:19:36,238 --> 01:19:38,039
This is tough.

2039
01:19:38,106 --> 01:19:39,674
-Time to decide.
-You must decide.

2040
01:19:39,741 --> 01:19:41,777
Please make your decision.

2041
01:19:41,843 --> 01:19:44,946
<i>Press one for Yoon Nam-no.</i>

2042
01:19:45,013 --> 01:19:46,248
<i>Press two for Kim Poong.</i>

2043
01:19:48,150 --> 01:19:49,551
Make your decision

2044
01:19:49,618 --> 01:19:51,119
and press the button.

2045
01:19:51,620 --> 01:19:53,955
-Here we go.
-Here we go.

2046
01:19:54,523 --> 01:19:55,457
Go.

2047
01:19:56,091 --> 01:19:57,125
-Did you choose one?
-Yes.

2048
01:19:57,192 --> 01:19:58,193
SHE HAS CHOSEN

2049
01:19:58,260 --> 01:20:00,495
Which one of you did she choose?

2050
01:20:00,562 --> 01:20:02,798
-I ran from him for ten months.
-The situation always changes.

2051
01:20:02,864 --> 01:20:05,300
Let's find out her choice. Show us.

2052
01:20:06,434 --> 01:20:08,069
Eats Like a Kid, Chae Jung-an Core.

2053
01:20:08,136 --> 01:20:12,207
Will Poong, who has won against Nam-no
every single time, take another victory?

2054
01:20:12,274 --> 01:20:15,043
Or will Nam-no win a star

2055
01:20:15,110 --> 01:20:17,813
against Poong for a change?

2056
01:20:17,879 --> 01:20:21,516
-Which chef did Jung-an choose?
-Who is it?

2057
01:20:21,583 --> 01:20:23,151
Jung-an's choice.

2058
01:20:23,218 --> 01:20:25,687
-Which chef did Jung-an choose?
-Who is it?

2059
01:20:25,754 --> 01:20:28,456
Who's going to win?

2060
01:20:28,523 --> 01:20:29,791
YOON NAM-NO WINS

2061
01:20:29,858 --> 01:20:30,959
Yoon Nam-no wins!

2062
01:20:31,726 --> 01:20:34,496
KIM POONG, SO WHAT?
CRY OVER MY SUJEBI WINS

2063
01:20:35,096 --> 01:20:37,566
NAM-NO HAS FINALLY WON!

2064
01:20:38,133 --> 01:20:40,368
It's Yoon Nam-no's first victory!

2065
01:20:40,435 --> 01:20:42,037
It's a moving moment.

2066
01:20:42,103 --> 01:20:43,839
He finally beat Kim Poong.

2067
01:20:43,905 --> 01:20:45,974
This is his first time.

2068
01:20:46,041 --> 01:20:49,511
-I teared up a little.
-He wins against Poong for the first time.

2069
01:20:49,578 --> 01:20:51,213
Is there a specific reason?

2070
01:20:51,279 --> 01:20:54,382
The moment I saw
that he was making oil <i>tteokbokki,</i>

2071
01:20:54,449 --> 01:20:57,819
it brought back my youthful days

2072
01:20:57,886 --> 01:20:59,955
and made me feel nostalgic.

2073
01:21:00,021 --> 01:21:04,159
Also, when I ate the cheese ball
made with mugwort <i>tteok,</i>

2074
01:21:04,226 --> 01:21:07,762
it made me feel wholesome,
like I'm a kind person.

2075
01:21:07,829 --> 01:21:09,464
I liked how lovely it was.

2076
01:21:10,031 --> 01:21:11,900
When I saw Poong's dish,

2077
01:21:11,967 --> 01:21:15,203
I already liked how it looked,

2078
01:21:15,270 --> 01:21:17,105
so I had high expectations.

2079
01:21:17,172 --> 01:21:21,676
But the flavor didn't match
what I expected, so that was a shame.

2080
01:21:21,743 --> 01:21:24,746
You should've been your usual self.

2081
01:21:24,813 --> 01:21:26,014
Well, today,

2082
01:21:26,514 --> 01:21:28,817
the fact that I finished it is a miracle.

2083
01:21:28,884 --> 01:21:30,385
-Really?
-That's true.

2084
01:21:30,452 --> 01:21:32,420
I didn't have enough time, so…

2085
01:21:32,487 --> 01:21:34,823
It didn't seem that hard to make.

2086
01:21:35,590 --> 01:21:36,758
THE 720,706TH HATE COMMENT

2087
01:21:36,825 --> 01:21:38,093
Until the end.

2088
01:21:38,159 --> 01:21:40,929
-Wow, that hater.
-Chef Nam-no.

2089
01:21:40,996 --> 01:21:43,465
You've won against Poong
for the first time.

2090
01:21:43,531 --> 01:21:46,368
I've been running from him
for the last ten months,

2091
01:21:46,434 --> 01:21:48,270
avoiding him.

2092
01:21:48,336 --> 01:21:51,106
I'm so glad to have broken
that losing streak

2093
01:21:51,172 --> 01:21:53,742
and I'll continue to win
four times and five times.

2094
01:21:54,943 --> 01:21:58,046
Nam-no has won three times in a row.

2095
01:21:58,113 --> 01:21:59,347
He's charging on.

2096
01:21:59,414 --> 01:22:01,316
-Please put a star on him.
-Good one, Poong.

2097
01:22:01,383 --> 01:22:03,351
He finally gets a big star.

2098
01:22:03,418 --> 01:22:05,220
-It's his fifth one!
-Wow.

2099
01:22:05,287 --> 01:22:07,222
Will she headbutt him?

2100
01:22:07,289 --> 01:22:08,523
Nam-no's fifth victory.

2101
01:22:08,590 --> 01:22:09,658
SCARED

2102
01:22:11,059 --> 01:22:13,328
-She might punch me like this.
-No.

2103
01:22:13,395 --> 01:22:14,362
I don't use my hand.

2104
01:22:15,030 --> 01:22:16,665
THEN WHAT… DO YOU USE?

2105
01:22:16,731 --> 01:22:20,302
-Congratulations on your fifth win!
-Congratulations.

2106
01:22:20,368 --> 01:22:23,371
COMING UP: PREVIEW FOR LEE SEON-MIN
AND CODE KUNST'S EPISODE

2107
01:22:23,872 --> 01:22:25,774
<i>He's the hottest potato at the moment,</i>

2108
01:22:25,840 --> 01:22:27,676
<i>who's living his glory days right now.</i>

2109
01:22:27,742 --> 01:22:30,045
The only thing left to do
was to be on <i>Chef & My Fridge.</i>

2110
01:22:30,111 --> 01:22:32,981
Please welcome
Lee Seon-min and CODE KUNST!

2111
01:22:33,048 --> 01:22:34,115
EDDIE'S HERE TOO

2112
01:22:34,182 --> 01:22:36,818
You're a regular cast member
on ten different channels.

2113
01:22:36,885 --> 01:22:38,787
For the brand reputation index,

2114
01:22:38,853 --> 01:22:40,789
he came in second after Yu Jae-seok.

2115
01:22:41,022 --> 01:22:43,024
THE FELLOW SINGLE MAN'S BRILLIANT GROWTH

2116
01:22:43,091 --> 01:22:45,293
Did you bring up Woni?

2117
01:22:45,360 --> 01:22:47,295
I took her to a fine dining restaurant.

2118
01:22:47,495 --> 01:22:48,596
MR. BIG SHOT, LEE SEON-MIN,

2119
01:22:49,764 --> 01:22:51,166
SHARES HIS FRIDGE WITH A TWIST

2120
01:22:51,232 --> 01:22:53,201
Can't I just remove the mold and eat it?

2121
01:22:53,268 --> 01:22:55,003
He eats the desiccant in seaweed packets.

2122
01:22:55,070 --> 01:22:56,471
It's pretty good.

2123
01:22:56,738 --> 01:22:58,073
THE HUMAN DISHWASHER EATS IT ALL

2124
01:22:58,306 --> 01:23:00,375
THE HOTTEST CHEFS ARE HERE TO COOK

2125
01:23:00,542 --> 01:23:03,078
FOR THE HUMAN DISHWASHER, LEE SEON-MIN

2126
01:23:03,144 --> 01:23:05,013
BOLD TOUCHES

2127
01:23:05,080 --> 01:23:06,281
AND DELICIOUS TECHNIQUES

2128
01:23:06,348 --> 01:23:07,282
THAT LOOKS GOOD

2129
01:23:07,349 --> 01:23:08,917
He's not using the potato.

2130
01:23:08,984 --> 01:23:10,452
You can't throw anything out!

2131
01:23:10,518 --> 01:23:11,820
He even eats moldy food!

2132
01:23:11,886 --> 01:23:13,154
-It's okay.
-I'll throw it in, geez.

2133
01:23:13,388 --> 01:23:14,255
CANINE HAS GONE EVIL

2134
01:23:14,522 --> 01:23:15,357
SLURP!

2135
01:23:15,423 --> 01:23:16,291
YUM!

2136
01:23:16,624 --> 01:23:17,993
WE HAVE OUR OWN HUMAN DISHWASHER TOO!

2137
01:23:18,059 --> 01:23:18,893
He's eating it all.

2138
01:23:18,960 --> 01:23:20,328
He's eating everything as he cooks.

2139
01:23:20,528 --> 01:23:22,163
HOLDING THE SHOW'S DISHWASHER IN CHECK

2140
01:23:22,397 --> 01:23:25,166
SEON-MIN SUCKS IN EVERYTHING

2141
01:23:25,233 --> 01:23:26,768
He's such son-in-law material.

2142
01:23:27,002 --> 01:23:29,037
SLURPING PASTA LIKE IT'S JAPCHAE

2143
01:23:29,104 --> 01:23:30,171
WHICH EMPTY DISH WILL CAPTURE

2144
01:23:30,372 --> 01:23:32,207
THE HUMAN DISHWASHER'S HEART?

2145
01:23:32,273 --> 01:23:33,641
<i>He eats so well.</i>

2146
01:23:33,708 --> 01:23:34,843
<i>He makes it look so good.</i>

2147
01:23:36,611 --> 01:23:39,647
-Man.
-What is it? Why are you surprised?

2148
01:23:39,714 --> 01:23:40,849
It's the taste of my kind.

2149
01:23:40,915 --> 01:23:42,784
-The taste of his kind?
-My kind.

2150
01:23:52,260 --> 01:23:57,032
Subtitle translation by: Eunsook Youn
